[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 1: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 1 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/dc6cfb60-619e-4713-8959-42f746d5a74e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course about the Czech Benoni, a fighting way to play against the queen's pawn! The Czech Benoni is an excellent way to catch your opponents off guard, get imbalanced positions, and steer things away from the typical queen's pawn structures. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White controls the d5-square. We'll challenge their center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. Now it's time for us to strike in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops by bringing out the knight. We now support the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White expands in the center. We continue by getting out our kingside pieces.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White wants to go for the Nge2 setup, so developing the bishop to d3 is on the board. Our b8-knight starts to reroute towards the kingside.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White applies the mentioned plan. Before rerouting, it is a good idea for us to gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.f4 {White goes for kingside activity immediately, which is a dubious plan. We'll capture on f4 and bear in mind that e5 can be a useful square later on for our d7-knight.} 8...exf4 9.Bxf4 {White recaptures. Let's claim a little more kingside space.} 9...h4 10.h3 {White stops our pawn from advancing. But this has weakened g3 somewhat. Let's target the dark squares with our f6-knight.} 10...Nh5 11.Bh2 {White retreats. The bishop on h2 is eyeing the d6-pawn and combined with Nb5, it could be weak. Hence we play the prophylactic a6, keeing the knight at bay.} 11...a6 12.O-O {White castles. Let's occupy the e5-square.} 12...Ne5 13.Nf4 {White wants to trade some pieces to ease the attack. We won't make it easy for them.} 13...Ng3 14.Rf2 {White covers the rook.} 14...g5 {And now we kick away the f4-knight. White's bishops are very passive. If White gets rid of our knight on g3, the h-file will be open for attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 1: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 1 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/dc6cfb60-619e-4713-8959-42f746d5a74e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course about the Czech Benoni, a fighting way to play against the queen's pawn! The Czech Benoni is an excellent way to catch your opponents off guard, get imbalanced positions, and steer things away from the typical queen's pawn structures. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White controls the d5-square. We'll challenge their center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. Now it's time for us to strike in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops by bringing out the knight. We now support the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White expands in the center. We continue by getting out our kingside pieces.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White wants to go for the Nge2 setup, so developing the bishop to d3 is on the board. Our b8-knight starts to reroute towards the kingside.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White applies the mentioned plan. Before rerouting, it is a good idea for us to gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.O-O {White castles here, which occurs in 50% of the games. Let's push even further!} 8...h4 9.h3 {White stops the h-pawn. We slowly but surely start to get our knights onto better squares.} 9...Nh5 10.a3 {White goes for the second most popular move, a3. The plan is to put pressure on our queenside. Our aim is to grab control over the kingside dark squares near the white king, so trading off the dark-squared bishops is a great plan here.} 10...Bg5 11.b4 {White initiates their plan.} 11...Rh6 {Via a rook lift, Black potentially adds an attacker to the kingside. The bishops will get traded, which plays into our hands as the f4-square will be used as an outpost.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 1: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 1 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/dc6cfb60-619e-4713-8959-42f746d5a74e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course about the Czech Benoni, a fighting way to play against the queen's pawn! The Czech Benoni is an excellent way to catch your opponents off guard, get imbalanced positions, and steer things away from the typical queen's pawn structures. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White controls the d5-square. We'll challenge their center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. Now it's time for us to strike in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops by bringing out the knight. We now support the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White expands in the center. We continue by getting out our kingside pieces.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White wants to go for the Nge2 setup, so developing the bishop to d3 is on the board. Our b8-knight starts to reroute towards the kingside.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White applies the mentioned plan. Before rerouting, it is a good idea for us to gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.O-O {White castles here, which occurs in 50% of the games. Let's push even further!} 8...h4 9.h3 {White stops the h-pawn. We slowly but surely start to get our knights onto better squares.} 9...Nh5 10.f4 {White creates a counterattack to not stay passive. In the future, we want to occupy the e5-square, so a trade is in place.} 10...exf4 11.Nxf4 {White recaptures with the knight. Now we have a beautiful outpost.} 11...Ng3 12.Rf2 {White saves the rook. Let's restrict White's pieces even further!} 12...g5 13.Nfe2 {The knight retreats to e2.} 13...Ne5 {And Black finally occupies the e5-square. Here, we can keep the king on e8 and enjoy a nice grip over the light-squares! The attack could continue with ...g4, which is complemented by the bishop on c8.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 1: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 1 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/dc6cfb60-619e-4713-8959-42f746d5a74e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course about the Czech Benoni, a fighting way to play against the queen's pawn! The Czech Benoni is an excellent way to catch your opponents off guard, get imbalanced positions, and steer things away from the typical queen's pawn structures. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White controls the d5-square. We'll challenge their center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. Now it's time for us to strike in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops by bringing out the knight. We now support the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White expands in the center. We continue by getting out our kingside pieces.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White wants to go for the Nge2 setup, so developing the bishop to d3 is on the board. Our b8-knight starts to reroute towards the kingside.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White applies the mentioned plan. Before rerouting, it is a good idea for us to gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.h3 {White goes for h3, the second most popular move in this position. We can achieve our ...h4-move.} 8...h4 9.Be3 {White develops the dark-squared bishop. Be3 could indicate that White will castle queenside, so we won't be going for ...Nh5 and ...g5 later. We'll bring the other knight to g6, starting with Nf8.} 9...Nf8 10.Qd2 {White sees our forces massing on the kingside and prepares to castle queenside.} 10...Ng6 11.O-O-O {White castles queenside. Looks like they are safe.} 11...a6 {We give ourselves the option of future line-opening with ...b5. The alternative plan of ...Nh7 and ...Bg5, playing to control the kingside dark squares, would be ineffectual since White's king hasn't castled kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 1: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 1 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/dc6cfb60-619e-4713-8959-42f746d5a74e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course about the Czech Benoni, a fighting way to play against the queen's pawn! The Czech Benoni is an excellent way to catch your opponents off guard, get imbalanced positions, and steer things away from the typical queen's pawn structures. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White controls the d5-square. We'll challenge their center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. Now it's time for us to strike in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops by bringing out the knight. We now support the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White expands in the center. We continue by getting out our kingside pieces.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White wants to go for the Nge2 setup, so developing the bishop to d3 is on the board. Our b8-knight starts to reroute towards the kingside.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White applies the mentioned plan. Before rerouting, it is a good idea for us to gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.O-O {White castles here, which occurs in 50% of the games. Let's push even further!} 8...h4 9.h3 {White stops the h-pawn. We slowly but surely start to get our knights onto better squares.} 9...Nh5 10.f4 {White creates a counterattack to not stay passive. In the future, we want to occupy the e5-square, so a trade is in place.} 10...exf4 11.Bxf4 {White recaptures with the bishop. We'll trade.} 11...Nxf4 12.Nxf4 {White recaptures.} 12...Ne5 {And we establish our strong central knight yet again. The plan now is to create a queenside counterplay, starting with ...a6. Going for ...g5 and ...g4 would be a mistaken plan here. We shouldn't open up our king since White has the open f-file and we've traded one of our defensive knights.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 1: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 1 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/dc6cfb60-619e-4713-8959-42f746d5a74e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to this course about the Czech Benoni, a fighting way to play against the queen's pawn! The Czech Benoni is an excellent way to catch your opponents off guard, get imbalanced positions, and steer things away from the typical queen's pawn structures. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White controls the d5-square. We'll challenge their center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. Now it's time for us to strike in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops by bringing out the knight. We now support the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White expands in the center. We continue by getting out our kingside pieces.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White wants to go for the Nge2 setup, so developing the bishop to d3 is on the board. Our b8-knight starts to reroute towards the kingside.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White applies the mentioned plan. Before rerouting, it is a good idea for us to gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.O-O {White castles here, which occurs in 50% of the games. Let's push even further!} 8...h4 9.h3 {White stops the h-pawn. We slowly but surely start to get our knights onto better squares.} 9...Nh5 10.Be3 {The third most popular move Be3...} 10...g5 {...is answered with ...g5! Going for the dark-squared grip yet again.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 2: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 2 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/26589aee-d4ac-45ed-8567-5743f9e82050/lines"]

1.d4 {For this second study, we'll explore some more continuations in the Bd3+Ne2 setup. These branches occur at move 8.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We'll play to challenge it.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. We'll strike back with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White supports the center. We'll develop our f8-bishop.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White gets the bishop out first before Nge2. The rerouting begins with ...Nbd7 next.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White places their knight to e2. We'll gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.h4 {This is a very rare move, but a great choice by White! For now, we stick to our rerouting plan.} 8...Nf8 9.f3 {White supports the e4-pawn. Let's fight for the f4-square, as per usual.} 9...Ng6 10.g3 {White covers the f4-square. We have to be flexible here, with plans on both sides of the board. Let's play a6.} 10...a6 11.Be3 {White develops. We'll support the ...b5 pawn break.} 11...Bd7 12.a4 {White stops ...b5, but we can reinforce it!} 12...Rb8 13.Qd2 {White is still deciding where to castle.} 13...Qc8 {And after ...Qc8 we control some kingside light-squares, but most importantly, we prepare a nice maneuver with ...Bd8 to ...Ba5, to control the queenside dark-squares!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 2: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 2 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/26589aee-d4ac-45ed-8567-5743f9e82050/lines"]

1.d4 {For this second study, we'll explore some more continuations in the Bd3+Ne2 setup. These branches occur at move 8.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We'll play to challenge it.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. We'll strike back with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White supports the center. We'll develop our f8-bishop.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White gets the bishop out first before Nge2. The rerouting begins with ...Nbd7 next.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White places their knight to e2. We'll gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.f3 {White supports their e4-pawn, the third most popular move. Even here, it is a good idea to continue expanding on the kingside.} 8...h4 9.Be3 {White brings the bishop to e3. We don't castle here. Instead, we'll reroute the knight via f8 to g6.} 9...Nf8 10.Qd2 {White prepares to castle queenside. The knight on g6 will fight for the crucial f4-square.} 10...Ng6 11.O-O-O {White castles. Ideally, we would want to prepare a queenside attack with ...b5.} 11...a6 12.g3 {White tries to open up the kingside. Let's prepare ...b5 even further.} 12...Bd7 13.f4 {White pushes, trying for kingside expansion...} 13...Ng4 {...but we'll ignore the push and bring our knight to g4. Getting rid of White's dark-squared bishop would be a great positional advantage. In the next few moves, we'll also strive for the ...b5-push.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 2: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 2 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/26589aee-d4ac-45ed-8567-5743f9e82050/lines"]

1.d4 {For this second study, we'll explore some more continuations in the Bd3+Ne2 setup. These branches occur at move 8.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We'll play to challenge it.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. We'll strike back with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White supports the center. We'll develop our f8-bishop.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White gets the bishop out first before Nge2. The rerouting begins with ...Nbd7 next.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White places their knight to e2. We'll gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.Bg5 {White goes for the dark-squared bishop development, but this is a mistake, allowing for a nice tactic with Nxd5.} 8...Nxd5 9.Bxe7 {White's bishop is hanging, so they trade.} 9...Nxe7 {And we can recapture back with the knight! With an extra pawn this is a great position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 2: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 2 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/26589aee-d4ac-45ed-8567-5743f9e82050/lines"]

1.d4 {For this second study, we'll explore some more continuations in the Bd3+Ne2 setup. These branches occur at move 8.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We'll play to challenge it.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. We'll strike back with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White supports the center. We'll develop our f8-bishop.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White gets the bishop out first before Nge2. The rerouting begins with ...Nbd7 next.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White places their knight to e2. We'll gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.Ng3 {White immediately jumps to the g3-square, threatening to jump to the f5-square, which we can easily prevent.} 8...g6 9.h4 {White blocks the h-pawn.} 9...Nh7 {We target the h4-pawn, which is now in trouble.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 2: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 2 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/26589aee-d4ac-45ed-8567-5743f9e82050/lines"]

1.d4 {For this second study, we'll explore some more continuations in the Bd3+Ne2 setup. These branches occur at move 8.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We'll play to challenge it.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. We'll strike back with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White supports the center. We'll develop our f8-bishop.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White gets the bishop out first before Nge2. The rerouting begins with ...Nbd7 next.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White places their knight to e2. We'll gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.Ng3 {White immediately jumps to the g3-square, threatening to jump to the f5-square, which we can easily prevent.} 8...g6 9.O-O {White goes for castling. Let's kick the knight from g3.} 9...h4 10.Nge2 {The knight must retreat.} 10...Nh5 {And after ...Nh5 we prepare the dark-squared invasion.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Bd3 and Ne2 Setups / Study 2: Fighting Bd3+Ne2 Part 2 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/85e8b7d5-b971-4b41-a6bf-6ab6d0e944c9/studies/26589aee-d4ac-45ed-8567-5743f9e82050/lines"]

1.d4 {For this second study, we'll explore some more continuations in the Bd3+Ne2 setup. These branches occur at move 8.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We'll play to challenge it.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White expands in the center. We'll strike back with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White supports the center. We'll develop our f8-bishop.} 5...Be7 6.Bd3 {White gets the bishop out first before Nge2. The rerouting begins with ...Nbd7 next.} 6...Nbd7 7.Nge2 {White places their knight to e2. We'll gain space on the kingside.} 7...h5 8.h4 {This is a very rare move, but a great choice by White! For now, we stick to our rerouting plan.} 8...Nf8 9.Ng3 {If Ng3 to attack the pawn on h5...} 9...Ng4 {...Ng4 and the the pawn on h4 is lost!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 1: The Popular Be2 Setup / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/54ebb758-3b59-4cb2-bf2b-a7860a22e70d/studies/eadc0e31-d59a-4e19-b653-f149343a1bf0/lines"]

1.d4 {For this chapter, we will be seeing more setups that are also high in popularity. We'll examine the very natural Nf3+Be2 or Bd3 setups.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central control by playing c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. Now we'll push in the center.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. We'll strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We'll develop our king's bishop and prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. This move will be the focus of this chapter. White develops the knight to the most active square, but in turn, blocks their f-pawn. Yet again, Black goes for the rerouting of the queen's knight.} 6...Nbd7 7.Be2 {Most often, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 42% of the time. White clearly states that they will castle kingside, without any f-pawn pushes yet, so we can castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Our bishop on e7 could use some breathing space!} 8...Ne8 9.Rb1 {White's final option is Rb1, with the idea to support a b-pawn advance. This is only played about 5% of the time, but still worth analyzing. Here, the g7-square will be used as a rerouting square as well.} 9...g6 10.Ne1 {White brings their knight back, looking to play Nd3 and push f4. We'll trade off White's strong dark-squared bishop!} 10...Bg5 11.Nd3 {White jumps the knight into d3. The threat is f4, so we have to go for the trade.} 11...Bxc1 12.Qxc1 {And when White recaptures, we'll place our queen on a square from where it has more scope.} 12...Qe7 13.f4 {The most critical and direct way for White to proceed is to advance f4. As per usual, Black frees up the e5-square here.} 13...exf4 14.Qxf4 {White takes back with the queen. The threat could be e5, which would open up lines for the attack. Preventing it is a good idea here.} 14...f6 15.Rf2 {Natural is now Rf2, preparing to triple on the semi-open f-file. Black now occupies the created e5 outpost.} 15...Ne5 16.Nxe5 {And when White takes our knight, we will recapture with the d-pawn, so ...f5 is possible in the future.} 16...dxe5 17.Qe3 {White moves their queen to safety, keeping eyes on the c5-pawn. Let's add some support to it.} 17...b6 18.Rbf1 {White doubles their rooks.} 18...Bd7 19.h4 {And if White tries to launch a kingside attack, as seen in the game Nielsen - Carlsen, 2007...} 19...Nd6 {We can simply go ...Nd6. Magnus went on to win the game. A strong plan is to double rooks and advance the f-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 1: The Popular Be2 Setup / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/54ebb758-3b59-4cb2-bf2b-a7860a22e70d/studies/eadc0e31-d59a-4e19-b653-f149343a1bf0/lines"]

1.d4 {For this chapter, we will be seeing more setups that are also high in popularity. We'll examine the very natural Nf3+Be2 or Bd3 setups.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central control by playing c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. Now we'll push in the center.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. We'll strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We'll develop our king's bishop and prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. This move will be the focus of this chapter. White develops the knight to the most active square, but in turn, blocks their f-pawn. Yet again, Black goes for the rerouting of the queen's knight.} 6...Nbd7 7.Be2 {Most often, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 42% of the time. White clearly states that they will castle kingside, without any f-pawn pushes yet, so we can castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Our bishop on e7 could use some breathing space!} 8...Ne8 9.Ne1 {Their idea is to play Nd3 and f4 before we can advance ...f5. Our dark-squared bishop isn't complementary to our structure, so we'll trade it off.} 9...Bg5 10.Bxg5 {White can also trade the bishops. We recapture.} 10...Qxg5 11.Qc1 {And when White tries to trade off our active queen, we should definitely decline this trade.} 11...Qe7 12.Nd3 {White improves their knight.} 12...g6 {And we play ...g6. The plan is simple - we'll move our knight to g7 and advance ...f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 1: The Popular Be2 Setup / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/54ebb758-3b59-4cb2-bf2b-a7860a22e70d/studies/eadc0e31-d59a-4e19-b653-f149343a1bf0/lines"]

1.d4 {For this chapter, we will be seeing more setups that are also high in popularity. We'll examine the very natural Nf3+Be2 or Bd3 setups.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central control by playing c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. Now we'll push in the center.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. We'll strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We'll develop our king's bishop and prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. This move will be the focus of this chapter. White develops the knight to the most active square, but in turn, blocks their f-pawn. Yet again, Black goes for the rerouting of the queen's knight.} 6...Nbd7 7.Be2 {Most often, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 42% of the time. White clearly states that they will castle kingside, without any f-pawn pushes yet, so we can castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Our bishop on e7 could use some breathing space!} 8...Ne8 9.a3 {White's second option is a3, preparing to push the b-pawn. This is played 20% of the time. We'll prepare a future ...f5.} 9...g6 10.Bh6 {The idea is to try to exploit our weakened dark-squares. We have to block the attack. This is played 33% of the time.} 10...Ng7 11.b4 {White advances their b-pawn. Let's support c5.} 11...b6 12.Qd2 {Logical is Qd2, activating the queen and connecting the rooks. An idea to kick away the annoying bishop on h6 is to go for ...Nf6 aimed at g4.} 12...Nf6 13.h3 {White goes h3, preventing ...Ng4. White has avoided ...Ng4, but we have another option of kicking away the bishop.} 13...Kh8 14.bxc5 {White captures the c5-pawn. We'll recapture , towards the center.} 14...bxc5 15.Rab1 {White takes the open b-file. Now Ng8 comes in handy to eject the strong h6-bishop.} 15...Ng8 16.Be3 {White brings their bishop back. Finally we can expand with f5.} 16...f5 17.exf5 {And when White takes...} 17...gxf5 {We can recapture with the g-pawn. We are doing very well here and ...f4 is a threat to win material. White does have control over the open b-file, but that's not relevant since there are no infiltration points.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 1: The Popular Be2 Setup / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/54ebb758-3b59-4cb2-bf2b-a7860a22e70d/studies/eadc0e31-d59a-4e19-b653-f149343a1bf0/lines"]

1.d4 {For this chapter, we will be seeing more setups that are also high in popularity. We'll examine the very natural Nf3+Be2 or Bd3 setups.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central control by playing c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. Now we'll push in the center.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. We'll strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We'll develop our king's bishop and prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. This move will be the focus of this chapter. White develops the knight to the most active square, but in turn, blocks their f-pawn. Yet again, Black goes for the rerouting of the queen's knight.} 6...Nbd7 7.Be2 {Most often, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 42% of the time. White clearly states that they will castle kingside, without any f-pawn pushes yet, so we can castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Our bishop on e7 could use some breathing space!} 8...Ne8 9.Be3 {White's third option is to simply develop the dark-squared bishop. This is played 10% of the time. We'll play g6, again planning the ...Ng7 and ...f5 expansion.} 9...g6 10.Bh6 {White proceeds with Bh6, looking to exploit the weakened dark-squares and attacking our rook The only way to save the rook is to block.} 10...Ng7 11.Qd2 {White goes Qd2, activating their queen and connecting the rooks. Let's use similar ideas to the previous line.} 11...Nf6 12.h3 {White goes h3, stopping ...Ng4. Now we free up the g8-square.} 12...Kh8 13.Ne1 {White brings their knight back, looking to play Nd3 and push f4.} 13...Ng8 {And we play ...Ng8. We've got the setup that we want and will look to proceed with ...f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 1: The Popular Be2 Setup / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/54ebb758-3b59-4cb2-bf2b-a7860a22e70d/studies/eadc0e31-d59a-4e19-b653-f149343a1bf0/lines"]

1.d4 {For this chapter, we will be seeing more setups that are also high in popularity. We'll examine the very natural Nf3+Be2 or Bd3 setups.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central control by playing c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. Now we'll push in the center.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. We'll strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We'll develop our king's bishop and prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. This move will be the focus of this chapter. White develops the knight to the most active square, but in turn, blocks their f-pawn. Yet again, Black goes for the rerouting of the queen's knight.} 6...Nbd7 7.Be2 {Most often, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 42% of the time. White clearly states that they will castle kingside, without any f-pawn pushes yet, so we can castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Our bishop on e7 could use some breathing space!} 8...Ne8 9.a3 {White's second option is a3, preparing to push the b-pawn. This is played 20% of the time. We'll prepare a future ...f5.} 9...g6 10.b4 {Most often, White follows up on their previous move with b4. The g7-square can now be used for our knight to prepare ...f5 in the future.} 10...Ng7 11.Be3 {White develops their bishop. Now White is threatening dxc5, as ...Nxc5 can be met with Bxc5, so let's support c5 even further.} 11...b6 12.bxc5 {White captures the c5-pawn. We'll recapture and bring our knight into the game.} 12...Nxc5 13.Bxc5 {White captures the knight.} 13...bxc5 {And we take back with the b-pawn. White's queenside advance didn't yield much. We've got a pleasant position and will proceed with ...f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 1: The Popular Be2 Setup / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/54ebb758-3b59-4cb2-bf2b-a7860a22e70d/studies/eadc0e31-d59a-4e19-b653-f149343a1bf0/lines"]

1.d4 {For this chapter, we will be seeing more setups that are also high in popularity. We'll examine the very natural Nf3+Be2 or Bd3 setups.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central control by playing c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. Now we'll push in the center.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. We'll strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We'll develop our king's bishop and prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. This move will be the focus of this chapter. White develops the knight to the most active square, but in turn, blocks their f-pawn. Yet again, Black goes for the rerouting of the queen's knight.} 6...Nbd7 7.Be2 {Most often, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 42% of the time. White clearly states that they will castle kingside, without any f-pawn pushes yet, so we can castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Our bishop on e7 could use some breathing space!} 8...Ne8 9.Ne1 {Their idea is to play Nd3 and f4 before we can advance ...f5. Our dark-squared bishop isn't complementary to our structure, so we'll trade it off.} 9...Bg5 10.Nd3 {Usually, White responds by bringing their knight to d3. We don't have to immediately trade on c1, so we make improving moves first.} 10...g6 11.f4 {White advances their f-pawn. It's important we trade on f4.} 11...exf4 12.Bxf4 {White recaptures with the bishop. Now we trade the bishops.} 12...Bxf4 13.Rxf4 {White recaptures with their rook.} 13...Qe7 {And now we play ...Qe7, activating our queen and preparing ...Ne5. The computer evaluation lingers around equality, but the position is very imbalanced. After ...Ne5, we can proceed with ...Bd7, ...Ng7 and ...Rae8. A timely ...f5 break is also on the cards.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 1: The Popular Be2 Setup / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/54ebb758-3b59-4cb2-bf2b-a7860a22e70d/studies/eadc0e31-d59a-4e19-b653-f149343a1bf0/lines"]

1.d4 {For this chapter, we will be seeing more setups that are also high in popularity. We'll examine the very natural Nf3+Be2 or Bd3 setups.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central control by playing c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. Now we'll push in the center.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. We'll strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We'll develop our king's bishop and prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. This move will be the focus of this chapter. White develops the knight to the most active square, but in turn, blocks their f-pawn. Yet again, Black goes for the rerouting of the queen's knight.} 6...Nbd7 7.Be2 {Most often, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 42% of the time. White clearly states that they will castle kingside, without any f-pawn pushes yet, so we can castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Our bishop on e7 could use some breathing space!} 8...Ne8 9.Ne1 {Their idea is to play Nd3 and f4 before we can advance ...f5. Our dark-squared bishop isn't complementary to our structure, so we'll trade it off.} 9...Bg5 10.f4 {White can also respond with f4. This is played 15% of the time, and should be captured.} 10...exf4 11.Bxf4 {White recaptures with the bishop. We continue to exchange.} 11...Bxf4 12.Rxf4 {White recaptures.} 12...Ne5 {And we jump our knight into e5. One major downside for White here is that there's no convenient way to challenge or trade off our knight. Nd3 loses the c4-pawn, and Nf3 loses the exchange to ...Ng6. Our plan is to proceed with ...Qe7, ...Bd7, and to try to advance on the queenside with our a and b pawns.} *

