1.d4 {For this chapter, we will be seeing more setups that are also high in popularity. We'll examine the very natural Nf3+Be2 or Bd3 setups.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central control by playing c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. Now we'll push in the center.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. We'll strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We'll develop our king's bishop and prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. This move will be the focus of this chapter. White develops the knight to the most active square, but in turn, blocks their f-pawn. Yet again, Black goes for the rerouting of the queen's knight.} 6...Nbd7 7.Be2 {Most often, White develops their light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 42% of the time. White clearly states that they will castle kingside, without any f-pawn pushes yet, so we can castle as well.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Our bishop on e7 could use some breathing space!} 8...Ne8 9.Rb1 {White's final option is Rb1, with the idea to support a b-pawn advance. This is only played about 5% of the time, but still worth analyzing. Here, the g7-square will be used as a rerouting square as well.} 9...g6 10.Bh6 {White can also go for Bh6, but we already know how to deal with this.} 10...Ng7 {We go Ng7, with the plan of ...Nf6 and either ...Ng4 or ...Kh8 & ...Ng8.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 2: Other Bishop Moves - Bd3 and Bg5 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/54ebb758-3b59-4cb2-bf2b-a7860a22e70d/studies/d3a1eede-b16a-4e78-9e5a-07a298c56300/lines"]

1.d4 {In this study, we will explore some other bishop moves White may have in the Czech Benoni: Bd3 and Bg5.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. It's time to push the e-pawn.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. Black now supports the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. We'll respond with ...Nbd7.} 6...Nbd7 7.Bg5 {White's third option in terms of popularity is to develop the dark-squared bishop. This is played 5% of the time. We'll castle.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {A better way for White to proceed is to develop their bishop to e2. This is played 35% of the time. We want to plant our knight on f4 and trade the bishops.} 8...Nh5 9.Bxe7 {White trades the bishops. We recapture.} 9...Qxe7 10.Nxe5 {White can also go for tactics by capturing the e5-pawn. This, however, isn't a good idea. We capture with our knight.} 10...Nxe5 11.Bxh5 {And when White takes our knight, we have a pawn break.} 11...f5 12.O-O {White moves their king to safety.} 12...f4 {And now we advance ...f4. This position is winning for Black! The kingside attack with ...Qh5 and the rook lift is going to be decisive.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 2: Other Bishop Moves - Bd3 and Bg5 / Variation 4"]
