1.d4 {In this study, we will explore some other bishop moves White may have in the Czech Benoni: Bd3 and Bg5.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. It's time to push the e-pawn.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. Black now supports the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. We'll respond with ...Nbd7.} 6...Nbd7 7.Bg5 {White's third option in terms of popularity is to develop the dark-squared bishop. This is played 5% of the time. We'll castle.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {A better way for White to proceed is to develop their bishop to e2. This is played 35% of the time. We want to plant our knight on f4 and trade the bishops.} 8...Nh5 9.Bxe7 {White trades the bishops. We recapture.} 9...Qxe7 10.O-O {Most often, White castles. Now, the f4-square is free.} 10...Nf4 11.g3 {White goes g3, looking to remove our knight from the f4-square. Retreating it would be too passive.} 11...Nxe2+ 12.Qxe2 {And when White recaptures...} 12...Nf6 {We can bring our knight to f6. We'll proceed by developing our bishop to either h3 or g4, and can plan to advance ...f5 in the future.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Nf3 and Be2/Bd3 Setups / Study 2: Other Bishop Moves - Bd3 and Bg5 / Variation 1"]
