1.d4 {In this study, we will explore some other bishop moves White may have in the Czech Benoni: Bd3 and Bg5.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. It's time to push the e-pawn.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their queenside knight, preparing to advance e4. Black now supports the center.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {And White develops their knight. We'll respond with ...Nbd7.} 6...Nbd7 7.Bd3 {White's second most popular choice is to develop the bishop to d3. This is played 30% of the time. We can simply castle here.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White also castles. Let's go for the more aggressive ...Nh5, aiming at the f4-square.} 8...Nh5 9.g3 {The most natural way to proceed is g3, simply covering the f4-square. Let's prepare the typical ...f5.} 9...g6 10.Bh6 {White proceeds with Bh6, looking to exploit the weakened dark-squares and attacking our rook. Another benefit of 8...Nh5 is that we can retreat to g7.} 10...Ng7 11.Qd2 {Logical is Qd2, activating the queen and connecting the rooks.} 11...Nf6 {And now we bring our knight to f6. Our plan here is to exploit White's weakened light-squares with ...Bh3, and go for ...Ng4 and ...f5.} *

