[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 6. f4 or 6. h3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/afcdfbbc-8b2e-4090-a725-36e6774fcb32/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third chapter! This chapter is all about White's other setups and sidelines. Let's take a look!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. We push forward in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We solidify our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.f4 {White goes for 6. f4, immediately striking at our center and trying to get a slightly more open position. This is played 17% of the time. The choice here is straightforward, as we once again aim to free up the e5-square.} 6...exf4 7.Bxf4 {White recaptures. Let's start rerouting our queen's knight.} 7...Nbd7 8.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. The d7-knight will have a good square on g6.} 8...Nf8 9.e5 {White's second option is to be confrontational and play 9. e5. This is played 20% of the time. Black must trade.} 9...dxe5 10.Nxe5 {White recaptures with the knight. Now we go for the classic, but effective knight reroute.} 10...Ng6 11.Nxg6 {White responds by trading knights. We'll recapture towards the center, activating our rook.} 11...hxg6 12.Be2 {White's third option is to develop the light-squared bishop to e2. This is played 26% of the time. ...Nh5 would be captured, so let's aim to trade the bishop in another way.} 12...Bd6 13.Qd2 {Usually, White responds with Qd2, simply supporting the f4-bishop. Nb5 could be a threat, so we'll prevent it with a6.} 13...a6 14.O-O-O {White castles queenside.} 14...O-O {And we castle the opposite way. The position is super imbalanced, but we've got clear attacking ideas. We can look to proceed with ...Bf5 and ...Re8. ...b5 is also always on the cards to open the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 6. f4 or 6. h3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/afcdfbbc-8b2e-4090-a725-36e6774fcb32/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third chapter! This chapter is all about White's other setups and sidelines. Let's take a look!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. We push forward in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We solidify our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.f4 {White goes for 6. f4, immediately striking at our center and trying to get a slightly more open position. This is played 17% of the time. The choice here is straightforward, as we once again aim to free up the e5-square.} 6...exf4 7.Bxf4 {White recaptures. Let's start rerouting our queen's knight.} 7...Nbd7 8.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. The d7-knight will have a good square on g6.} 8...Nf8 9.Bd3 {Most often, White proceeds by developing their bishop to d3. This happens over half the time. We bring our knight to g6 with tempo on the f4-bishop.} 9...Ng6 10.Bg3 {White slides the bishop back. White's dark-squared bishop is a target almost in every Benoni postion.} 10...Nh5 11.Bf2 {White moves their bishop back. Our little knight dance allows us to grip the f4-square with both of them.} 11...Nhf4 12.O-O {The most logical way to proceed is to castle, defending the g2-pawn. Here, we opt for a positional approach, pinning the f3-knight.} 12...Bg4 13.h3 {White challenges our bishop. But our point was to trade off the f3-knight, so as to weaken White's control over e5.} 13...Bxf3 14.Qxf3 {White recaptures with the queen. ...Ne5 looks like a great move, but the f4-knight would fall, so first we capture on d3.} 14...Nxd3 15.Qxd3 {And when White recaptures.} 15...O-O {We can simply castle our king. Our position is pleasant and relatively easy to play. We'll look to proceed with ...Bf6, followed by ...Ne5 and ...a6.} *

