1.d4 {Welcome to the third chapter! This chapter is all about White's other setups and sidelines. Let's take a look!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. We push forward in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We solidify our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.f4 {White goes for 6. f4, immediately striking at our center and trying to get a slightly more open position. This is played 17% of the time. The choice here is straightforward, as we once again aim to free up the e5-square.} 6...exf4 7.Bxf4 {White recaptures. Let's start rerouting our queen's knight.} 7...Nbd7 8.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. The d7-knight will have a good square on g6.} 8...Nf8 9.e5 {White's second option is to be confrontational and play 9. e5. This is played 20% of the time. Black must trade.} 9...dxe5 10.Nxe5 {White recaptures with the knight. Now we go for the classic, but effective knight reroute.} 10...Ng6 11.Nxg6 {White responds by trading knights. We'll recapture towards the center, activating our rook.} 11...hxg6 12.Bd3 {Most often, White proceeds by developing their bishop to d3. This is played 36% of the time. Let's target the dark-squared bishop.} 12...Nh5 13.Be3 {White moves the bishop to safety, but this allows for a nice tactic.} 13...Ng3 14.Rg1 {White moves their rook to safety.} 14...Nf5 {And we simply retreat to e5. We target the dark-squared bishop, White cannot castle kingside, and our rook has an open h-file.} *

