1.d4 {Welcome to the third chapter! This chapter is all about White's other setups and sidelines. Let's take a look!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. We push forward in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We solidify our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.f4 {White goes for 6. f4, immediately striking at our center and trying to get a slightly more open position. This is played 17% of the time. The choice here is straightforward, as we once again aim to free up the e5-square.} 6...exf4 7.Bxf4 {White recaptures. Let's start rerouting our queen's knight.} 7...Nbd7 8.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. The d7-knight will have a good square on g6.} 8...Nf8 9.e5 {White's second option is to be confrontational and play 9. e5. This is played 20% of the time. Black must trade.} 9...dxe5 10.Nxe5 {White recaptures with the knight. Now we go for the classic, but effective knight reroute.} 10...Ng6 11.Nxg6 {White responds by trading knights. We'll recapture towards the center, activating our rook.} 11...hxg6 12.d6 {White can also go for the very natural d6, attacking our bishop. This, however, is inaccurate!} 12...Nh5 {We've got the cold shower ...Nh5, counterattacking White's bishop. Black is better in this position. If White now takes our bishop, we can recapture with the queen and follow up by taking White's bishop.} *

