[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 6. f4 or 6. h3 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/afcdfbbc-8b2e-4090-a725-36e6774fcb32/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third chapter! This chapter is all about White's other setups and sidelines. Let's take a look!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. We push forward in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We solidify our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.h3 {Our second move of interest in this study is 6. h3, simply preventing our pieces from accessing g4. This is played 8% of the time. We'll continue with our queen's knight plan.} 6...Nbd7 7.g4 {White can also push their g-pawn. This is only played 8% of the time, but worth investigating as it's the most confrontational approach.} 7...Nf8 {We'll respond ...Nf8, looking to bring our knight over to g6. Our plan in this position is to play on the dark-squares. This means that we want to go for ...h6 and maneuver our knights to h4 & g5. If White now goes g5, we can respond with ...Ng8 and White's g5-pawn becomes a liability.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 1: White Plays 6. f4 or 6. h3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/afcdfbbc-8b2e-4090-a725-36e6774fcb32/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third chapter! This chapter is all about White's other setups and sidelines. Let's take a look!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll challenge their central pawns.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking even more space. We push forward in the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We solidify our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We prepare to castle.} 5...Be7 6.h3 {Our second move of interest in this study is 6. h3, simply preventing our pieces from accessing g4. This is played 8% of the time. We'll continue with our queen's knight plan.} 6...Nbd7 7.Bd3 {White's second option is Bd3. This is played 20% of the time. The idea is to develop the knight to e2, castle kingside and advance f4. We continue with our knight plan.} 7...Nf8 8.Nge2 {White develops their knight, preparing kingside castling. We bring our knight to g6.} 8...Ng6 9.O-O {White castles their king.} 9...h5 {And we don't respond by castling, but rather advance our h-pawn! Our plan in this position is to push ...h4 and bring our f6-knight to h5. If White now advances f4, we can take the pawn and play ...Ne5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 6. g3 or 6. Bg5 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/3093ed93-741f-43c9-b2ac-0efc0045860e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! For this study, we'll be taking a look at g3 and Bg5 on move six. White opens with 1. d4. We respond with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We go for the Benoni with ...c5!} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking more space. We play 3...e5, the Czech Benoni.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We proceed with ...d6, freeing up the d7-square for our knight and opening our light-squared bishop.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We develop our dark-squared bishop and prepare castling. As mentioned before, this second study will cover 6. g3 and 6. Bg5.} 5...Be7 6.g3 {White goes 6. g3, preparing to fianchetto their bishop. This is played 7% of the time. Not much changes about our approach, although another cool option is 6...Na6, which I contemplated showing you in the video, but opted not to. We simply castle. Our plan in these positions will revolve around the ...f5 break.} 6...O-O 7.Bg2 {White fianchettoes their bishop. We'll proceed with ...Nbd7.} 7...Nbd7 8.Nge2 {White develops their knight, preparing kingside castling. Now we go ...a6, covering b5.} 8...a6 9.a4 {White plays a4, making sure that there are never any ...b5 breaks. And we go for ...Ne8. This is reminiscent of the first chapter. Our plan is to go ...g6, ...Ng7 and advance the f-pawn.} 9...Ne8 10.O-O {White castles their king. We'll go ...g6, preparing ...Ng7.} 10...g6 11.Bh6 {White proceeds with Bh6, looking to exploit the weakened dark-squares and attacking our rook. We have to respond with ...Ng7 in order not to lose the f8-rook. If White now allows it, we'll go ...Bg5 and trade the h6-bishop.} 11...Ng7 12.Qd2 {White goes Qd2, activating their queen and preventing ...Bg5. Now we play ...Nf6, looking to go after White's bishop with ...Ng4.} 12...Nf6 13.h3 {White goes h3, stopping ...Ng4.} 13...Kh8 {And now we play ...Kh8. Our plan here is simple - bring the knight back to g8 and push ...f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 2: White Plays 6. g3 or 6. Bg5 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/3093ed93-741f-43c9-b2ac-0efc0045860e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the second study! For this study, we'll be taking a look at g3 and Bg5 on move six. White opens with 1. d4. We respond with 1...Nf6.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We go for the Benoni with ...c5!} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking more space. We play 3...e5, the Czech Benoni.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We proceed with ...d6, freeing up the d7-square for our knight and opening our light-squared bishop.} 4...d6 5.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. We develop our dark-squared bishop and prepare castling. As mentioned before, this second study will cover 6. g3 and 6. Bg5.} 5...Be7 6.Bg5 {The second move of interest in this study is 6. Bg5, simply developing the dark-squared bishop. This, however, is a mistake!} 6...Nxd5 {Against 6. Bg5, we can go ...Nxd5! This move simply wins a pawn and gives us great winning chances.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes En Passant (4.dxe6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/2c0ea3bb-815c-49fb-af92-838eec427fbd/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third study! In this study, we'll be taking a look at what to do if White captures en passant after we advance 3...e5.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll play the Benoni with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking more space. We'll stake a claim to the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.dxe6 {The subject of this study will be White taking en passant. This really isn't great as it enables us to quickly take over the center. Naturally, we'll recapture with the f-pawn.} 4...fxe6 5.Nf3 {White develops the g1-knight. This is played 15% of the time. We'll push forward with d5.} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White captures the d-pawn, and we'll take back with the pawn.} 6...exd5 7.Bg5 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to g5, pinning our knight. We'll have to break this pin.} 7...Be7 8.e3 {White goes e3, covering the d4-square and opening the bishop. Let's get the king out of the center.} 8...O-O 9.Nc3 {White goes Nc3, threatening to take on f6 and win the d5-pawn. We now add support to the d5-pawn by developing.} 9...Be6 10.Be2 {White develops their bishop, preparing kingside castling. Let's get the last minor piece into the game.} 10...Nc6 11.O-O {White moves their king to safety.} 11...Qd7 {And we go ...Qd7, improving our queen and connecting the rooks. We're slightly better because of the space advantage in the center. Short-term, we can look to proceed with moves like ...h6 and ...Rad8.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 3: White Takes En Passant (4.dxe6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/2c0ea3bb-815c-49fb-af92-838eec427fbd/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third study! In this study, we'll be taking a look at what to do if White captures en passant after we advance 3...e5.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking more space in the center. We'll play the Benoni with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking more space. We'll stake a claim to the center with e5.} 3...e5 4.dxe6 {The subject of this study will be White taking en passant. This really isn't great as it enables us to quickly take over the center. Naturally, we'll recapture with the f-pawn.} 4...fxe6 5.Nc3 {White develops the b1-knight. This is played over half the time. Now we can establish a strong central structure with d5.} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White captures the d-pawn. We should recapture with the e-pawn, as other moves are simply weakening.} 6...exd5 7.Bg5 {White goes Bg5, threatening to take on f6 and win the d5-pawn. We can deal with this threat and gain a tempo with d4.} 7...d4 8.Ne4 {White responds by jumping their knight into e4, utilizing the pin. We can simply continue to develop, as there aren't any threats resulting from the pin.} 8...Bf5 9.Nxf6+ {White trades the knights, and we recapture.} 9...gxf6 10.Bd2 {White retreats their bishop. Let's continue with development.} 10...Nc6 11.Nf3 {White also develops their knight.} 11...Qb6 {And we bring our queen out. This position is excellent. We're better developed and have a significant space advantage. We'll look to proceed with ...O-O-O.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 4: White Pins Our Knight (5. Bg5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/4f1ddb3e-59a2-4709-b992-178e9b1f49f5/lines"]

1.d4 {For the final study in chapter 3, we'll be taking a look at the only notable sideline on move five - 5. Bg5. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking space in the center. We'll play the Benoni with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking more space. We'll stake a claim to the center by pushing e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.Bg5 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to g5, pinning our knight. This is the only notable sideline on move 5, and occurs 8% of the time. We simply break the pin with a logical developing move.} 5...Be7 6.Nf3 {White develops their other knight. This is played 17% of the time. Let's simply castle here.} 6...O-O 7.e3 {Seeing that e4 can be met with ...Nxd5, White goes for e3. Now ...Nxd5 tricks don't work, but we want to trade off the bishops anyway.} 7...Ne8 8.Bxe7 {White trades the bishops.} 8...Qxe7 {And we recapture. We've got a solid position and will look to proceed with ...f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 4: White Pins Our Knight (5. Bg5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/4f1ddb3e-59a2-4709-b992-178e9b1f49f5/lines"]

1.d4 {For the final study in chapter 3, we'll be taking a look at the only notable sideline on move five - 5. Bg5. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking space in the center. We'll play the Benoni with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking more space. We'll stake a claim to the center by pushing e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.Bg5 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to g5, pinning our knight. This is the only notable sideline on move 5, and occurs 8% of the time. We simply break the pin with a logical developing move.} 5...Be7 6.Bxf6 {White captures the f6-knight. This is played 21% of the time. We recapture with the bishop.} 6...Bxf6 7.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. They've handed their dark squares to us on a silver plate! Let's exploit them.} 7...Bg5 8.Nf3 {White develops their knight and targets our bishop.} 8...Bh6 {And we slide the bishop back. We've got a pleasant position and a simple plan to castle and push ...f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: Other White Setups & Sidelines / Study 4: White Pins Our Knight (5. Bg5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/51709fe4-ab17-423d-84e1-4c7fd8f54929/studies/4f1ddb3e-59a2-4709-b992-178e9b1f49f5/lines"]

1.d4 {For the final study in chapter 3, we'll be taking a look at the only notable sideline on move five - 5. Bg5. Let's get to it!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White advances their c-pawn, taking space in the center. We'll play the Benoni with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White responds by advancing their d-pawn, taking more space. We'll stake a claim to the center by pushing e5.} 3...e5 4.Nc3 {White develops their knight. We strengthen our center with d6.} 4...d6 5.Bg5 {White develops their dark-squared bishop to g5, pinning our knight. This is the only notable sideline on move 5, and occurs 8% of the time. We simply break the pin with a logical developing move.} 5...Be7 6.e4 {White advances their e-pawn. This is played 40% of the time. We've seen this position in Study 2. 6. e4 is actually a serious mistake!} 6...Nxd5 {We can meet 6. e4 with ...Nxd5! With this extra pawn we should have excellent winning chances.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 1: The Most Popular Sideline (3.Nc3) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/c255116b-ffe1-4edf-a56b-5fce685e5196/lines"]

1.d4 {You are now ready to start checking out some early sidelines. We will begin with third moves White could go for, starting with 3. Nc3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies more space. We'll reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to control some central squares. Now it's the right time to trade in the center.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We can develop and gain a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd1 {White retreats to d1. Let's open up our dark-squared bishop and prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Bg5 {White develops and pins our knight. Let's develop and pin as well.} 6...Bb4 7.Nf3 {White brings out the kingside knight. We can now weaken White's structure.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.bxc3 {White recaptures. Let's ask the bishop a question.} 8...h6 9.Bh4 {White retreats. Now it's time to start attacking the weakened pawns.} 9...Qa5 10.Qc2 {White defends c3.} 10...b6 {After ...b6 we can introduce an attacker to the c4-pawn with ...Ba6!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 1: The Most Popular Sideline (3.Nc3) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/c255116b-ffe1-4edf-a56b-5fce685e5196/lines"]

1.d4 {You are now ready to start checking out some early sidelines. We will begin with third moves White could go for, starting with 3. Nc3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies more space. We'll reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to control some central squares. Now it's the right time to trade in the center.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We can develop and gain a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd1 {White retreats to d1. Let's open up our dark-squared bishop and prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.e4 {White immediately grabs space with e4, the third most popular move in this position. We'll counter by pinning the knight.} 6...Bb4 7.Bd3 {White develops the bishop and protects e4. Now we can expand in the center.} 7...d5 8.cxd5 {White trades. We recapture.} 8...exd5 9.exd5 {White trades again. We'll take with the queen, hitting g2.} 9...Qxd5 10.Nf3 {White prepares to castle, and covers the g2-pawn. We'll increase the pressure by pinning the f3-knight.} 10...Bg4 11.O-O {White hides the king. Now the c3-knight is unpinned, and attacking our queen, so we will eliminate it.} 11...Bxc3 12.bxc3 {White recaptures.} 12...O-O-O {We have finished the opening stage and White is under pressure down the d-file. We could consider exchanging queens and entering an ending where the white c-pawn will be a long-term weakness.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 1: The Most Popular Sideline (3.Nc3) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/c255116b-ffe1-4edf-a56b-5fce685e5196/lines"]

1.d4 {You are now ready to start checking out some early sidelines. We will begin with third moves White could go for, starting with 3. Nc3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies more space. We'll reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to control some central squares. Now it's the right time to trade in the center.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We can develop and gain a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd1 {White retreats to d1. Let's open up our dark-squared bishop and prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nf3 {White brings the knight out to f3. We'll expand in the center with d5.} 6...d5 7.cxd5 {White trades, and we'll recapture.} 7...exd5 8.Bg5 {White pins the knight, which also undermines our d5-pawn. In response, we'll push forward, hitting the c3-knight.} 8...d4 9.Ne4 {White adds pressure to the knight on f6. But this allows a nice tactic!} 9...Nxe4 10.Bxd8 {White grabs our queen.} 10...Bb4+ {Check, and White has to give up their queen in return.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 1: The Most Popular Sideline (3.Nc3) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/c255116b-ffe1-4edf-a56b-5fce685e5196/lines"]

1.d4 {You are now ready to start checking out some early sidelines. We will begin with third moves White could go for, starting with 3. Nc3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies more space. We'll reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to control some central squares. Now it's the right time to trade in the center.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We can develop and gain a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd1 {White retreats to d1. Let's open up our dark-squared bishop and prepare ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nf3 {White brings the knight out to f3. We'll expand in the center with d5.} 6...d5 7.cxd5 {White trades, and we'll recapture.} 7...exd5 8.e3 {White opens up the light-squared bishop. Now Black has the option to indirectly fight for the center by pinning the c3-knight.} 8...Bb4 9.Bd2 {White breaks the pin, so let's simply castle.} 9...O-O 10.Be2 {White prepares to castle. We will simply play improving moves aimed at controlling the center.} 10...Re8 11.O-O {White castles.} 11...a6 {...a6 prevents White's knight from jumping to b5. Our plan is to develop the bishop to g4 and create a battery with our dark-squared bishop and our queen aimed towards White's king.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 1: The Most Popular Sideline (3.Nc3) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/c255116b-ffe1-4edf-a56b-5fce685e5196/lines"]

1.d4 {You are now ready to start checking out some early sidelines. We will begin with third moves White could go for, starting with 3. Nc3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies more space. We'll reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to control some central squares. Now it's the right time to trade in the center.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We can develop and gain a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd2 {By moving the queen to d2, White's idea is to play b3 with Bb2. The idea of ...d5 is still relevant.} 5...e6 6.Nf3 {White develops.} 6...d5 7.cxd5 {White trades. Black should recapture with the pawn.} 7...exd5 8.e3 {The pawn controls our d4-push. ...Bb4 will indirectly fight for the center.} 8...Bb4 9.a3 {White attacks the bishop. We can create a higher level threat!} 9...Ne4 10.Qc2 {White saves their queen. Now it's time to trade the bishop off, creating a weakness on c3.} 10...Bxc3+ 11.bxc3 {White recaptures.} 11...O-O {And we can castle here. We can develop the bishop to f5 or g4. After that, our rooks will have good scope on the e and c-files.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 1: The Most Popular Sideline (3.Nc3) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/c255116b-ffe1-4edf-a56b-5fce685e5196/lines"]

1.d4 {You are now ready to start checking out some early sidelines. We will begin with third moves White could go for, starting with 3. Nc3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies more space. We'll reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to control some central squares. Now it's the right time to trade in the center.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We can develop and gain a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd3 {White brings the queen to d3, keeping some activity but not retreating entirely. This is the third most popular move, and the plan is still ...d5.} 5...e6 6.Nf3 {White develops. We'll play the thematic d5.} 6...d5 7.cxd5 {White trades. We recapture with the pawn.} 7...exd5 8.Bg5 {White pins our knight on f6, putting pressure on the d5-pawn. We can save it by pushing.} 8...d4 9.Ne4 {White jumps to the e4-square. To keep the initiative, Black should play actively and gain some tempi.} 9...Bb4+ 10.Nfd2 {White blocks with the knight.} 10...Be7 {We retreat, with the plan of castling kingside and attacking White with ...Nb4.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 1: The Most Popular Sideline (3.Nc3) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/c255116b-ffe1-4edf-a56b-5fce685e5196/lines"]

1.d4 {You are now ready to start checking out some early sidelines. We will begin with third moves White could go for, starting with 3. Nc3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies more space. We'll reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to control some central squares. Now it's the right time to trade in the center.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We can develop and gain a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Qh4 {White moves to h4, intending to put some pressure on our kingside. Black has a counterplay resource here, exploiting the queen having wandered over to h4.} 5...Nb4 6.Rb1 {White moves the rook to avoid the fork on c2. Let's continue playing actively, and open the center with d5.} 6...d5 7.cxd5 {White trades. We'll recapture with the f6-knight, leaving the b4-knight with the option of a later check on c2.} 7...Nfxd5 8.Bd2 {White supports the knight on c3. We'll develop and attack the misplaced rook on b1.} 8...Bf5 9.e4 {White expands in the center and defends the rook. Black has to trade now.} 9...Nxc3 10.Bxc3 {White recaptures. But we can give a check on c2 and displace the white king.} 10...Nc2+ 11.Ke2 {The king moves.} 11...Be6 {...Bc4 is coming and White's king is not safe. We can even grab the pawn on a2 and expand with ...e5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 1: The Most Popular Sideline (3.Nc3) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/c255116b-ffe1-4edf-a56b-5fce685e5196/lines"]

1.d4 {You are now ready to start checking out some early sidelines. We will begin with third moves White could go for, starting with 3. Nc3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies more space. We'll reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nc3 {White develops the knight to control some central squares. Now it's the right time to trade in the center.} 3...cxd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures with the queen. We can develop and gain a tempo.} 4...Nc6 5.Qh4 {White moves to h4, intending to put some pressure on our kingside. Black has a counterplay resource here, exploiting the queen having wandered over to h4.} 5...Nb4 6.Kd1 {If White moves the king...} 6...d5 {We'll open up the center with ...d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 2: A Transposition to the English (3.Nf3) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/922ec5bd-d392-4878-9b34-1972daaa6de7/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study covers 3. Nf3. Let's check it out!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {White supports the pawn with the knight. We'll take on d4.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. We'll play aggressively, and challenge the central knight directly with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.Nb5 {White aims at the d6-weakness, which is played in 25% of the games. This allows us to strike in the center!} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White captures. Recapturing would fall for a tactic, so we'll go for rapid development instead.} 6...Bc5 7.d6 {White tries to disrupt our development by pushing the d-pawn further. We can completely ignore this.} 7...O-O 8.Nc7 {White thinks that they've trapped our rook, but we can generate a strong counter-attack!} 8...Ne4 9.e3 {White blocks our bishop. Let's keep the initiative here.} 9...Bb4+ 10.Bd2 {White blocks the check. Now we trade on d2.} 10...Nxd2 11.Nxd2 {White recaptures, but blocks the queen. We can now capture on d6, and focus on the pinned d2-knight.} 11...Qxd6 12.Nxa8 {White captures our rook...} 12...Rd8 {...but now their knight on d2 is lost! And with that their king safety as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 2: A Transposition to the English (3.Nf3) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/922ec5bd-d392-4878-9b34-1972daaa6de7/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study covers 3. Nf3. Let's check it out!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {White supports the pawn with the knight. We'll take on d4.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. We'll play aggressively, and challenge the central knight directly with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.Nb3 {White retreats to b3, the least played move. As this isn't a very active square for the knight, we will again break in the center with d5.} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White trades. We'll recapture, offering an exchange of queens.} 6...Qxd5 7.Qxd5 {White accepts. Our knight comes into the center of the board.} 7...Nxd5 8.e4 {So White kicks it out. But we can move to b4, and threaten a fork on c2.} 8...Nb4 9.Na3 {White covers it. We'll get our other knight out.} 9...N8c6 10.Bb5 {White develops as well, pinning our knight. We'll attack the bishop.} 10...a6 11.Bxc6+ {White trades, as retreating would just lose a tempo. We take back with the knight.} 11...Nxc6 12.O-O {White castles.} 12...Be6 {e6 is a very nice square for the light-squared bishop. We can bring the other bishop to e7 and castle with a very comfortable queenless middlegame.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 2: A Transposition to the English (3.Nf3) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/922ec5bd-d392-4878-9b34-1972daaa6de7/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study covers 3. Nf3. Let's check it out!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {White supports the pawn with the knight. We'll take on d4.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. We'll play aggressively, and challenge the central knight directly with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.Nf3 {White retreats the knight to f3, which is the second most common move, attacking our pawn. We'll develop and defend it.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White continues their development. We'll pin the knight.} 6...Bb4 7.Bd2 {White breaks the pin. We castle.} 7...O-O 8.a3 {White attacks our bishop instead. We'll capture on c3.} 8...Bxc3 9.Bxc3 {White recaptures with the bishop. We'll defend our strong central pawn.} 9...Qe7 10.e3 {White opens up the light-squared bishop.} 10...Rd8 {And the rook slides to d8 in order to support the d5-push!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 2: A Transposition to the English (3.Nf3) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/922ec5bd-d392-4878-9b34-1972daaa6de7/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study covers 3. Nf3. Let's check it out!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {White supports the pawn with the knight. We'll take on d4.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. We'll play aggressively, and challenge the central knight directly with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.Nb5 {White aims at the d6-weakness, which is played in 25% of the games. This allows us to strike in the center!} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White captures. Recapturing would fall for a tactic, so we'll go for rapid development instead.} 6...Bc5 7.e3 {White blocks the diagonal. Let's get the king into safety.} 7...O-O 8.N5c3 {White backs off the knight. The idea is to push e4 and gain total control over the center! We cannot allow this.} 8...e4 9.Be2 {White prepares to castle. Now we continue our development.} 9...Nbd7 10.O-O {White castles. ...e4 also allowed us to create a strong outpost for our knight.} 10...Ne5 11.Nd2 {White develops.} 11...Bf5 {The pawn on e4 is a strong asset that gives us a space advantage. We can target the weak d5-pawn next!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 2: A Transposition to the English (3.Nf3) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/922ec5bd-d392-4878-9b34-1972daaa6de7/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study covers 3. Nf3. Let's check it out!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {White supports the pawn with the knight. We'll take on d4.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. We'll play aggressively, and challenge the central knight directly with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.Nb5 {White aims at the d6-weakness, which is played in 25% of the games. This allows us to strike in the center!} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White captures. Recapturing would fall for a tactic, so we'll go for rapid development instead.} 6...Bc5 7.N5c3 {The knight retreats to c3, the second most popular move. As per the previous line, we'll get the king into safety first.} 7...O-O 8.g3 {White wants to fianchetto their light-squared bishop. The f2-pawn is a bit soft as e3 hasn't been played!} 8...Ng4 9.e3 {White blocks our dark-squared bishop, but weakens the light-squares around their king. To exploit White's weaknesses, a pawn break could be useful.} 9...f5 10.Be2 {White stops our pawn break idea, so let's just develop.} 10...Nd7 11.O-O {White castles.} 11...Qe8 {And after ...Qe8, we'll be able to play ...Qh5 and introduce the queen into the kingside attack.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 2: A Transposition to the English (3.Nf3) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/922ec5bd-d392-4878-9b34-1972daaa6de7/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study covers 3. Nf3. Let's check it out!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {White supports the pawn with the knight. We'll take on d4.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. We'll play aggressively, and challenge the central knight directly with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.Nf3 {White retreats the knight to f3, which is the second most common move, attacking our pawn. We'll develop and defend it.} 5...Nc6 6.Nc3 {White continues their development. We'll pin the knight.} 6...Bb4 7.Bd2 {White breaks the pin. We castle.} 7...O-O 8.e3 {White opens up the bishop and covers the d4-square. We gain space with ...e4, attacking the f3-knight.} 8...e4 9.Nd4 {White jumps to d4. We'll exchange knights.} 9...Nxd4 10.exd4 {White recaptures...} 10...d5 {...and we build a strong center with ...d5. The light-squared bishop will serve as a protector of our center.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 2: A Transposition to the English (3.Nf3) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/922ec5bd-d392-4878-9b34-1972daaa6de7/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study covers 3. Nf3. Let's check it out!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {White supports the pawn with the knight. We'll take on d4.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. We'll play aggressively, and challenge the central knight directly with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.Nc2 {White retreats the knight to c2, which is played in almost 20% of the games. We'll break in the center with d5.} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White trades. We offer an exchange of queens.} 6...Qxd5 7.Qxd5 {White trades, and we recapture, bringing our knight into the center of the board.} 7...Nxd5 8.e4 {White attacks our knight. But we can leap to b4 and keep generating threats.} 8...Nb4 9.Nxb4 {White recaptures. We'll take back, giving check.} 9...Bxb4+ 10.Bd2 {White blocks. We'll exchange bishops.} 10...Bxd2+ 11.Nxd2 {White recaptures.} 11...Ke7 {Our development will be very natural from now, with the rook immediately taking control over the open d-file and our minor pieces coming out! The central configuration slightly favours our bishop, as it is not restricted by its own pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 2: A Transposition to the English (3.Nf3) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/922ec5bd-d392-4878-9b34-1972daaa6de7/lines"]

1.d4 {The second study covers 3. Nf3. Let's check it out!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White occupies the center. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.Nf3 {White supports the pawn with the knight. We'll take on d4.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {White recaptures. We'll play aggressively, and challenge the central knight directly with our e-pawn.} 4...e5 5.Nb5 {White aims at the d6-weakness, which is played in 25% of the games. This allows us to strike in the center!} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {White captures. Recapturing would fall for a tactic, so we'll go for rapid development instead.} 6...Bc5 7.N1c3 {White brings out their knight from b1, which is the third most popular move. Let's get our king into safety.} 7...O-O 8.e3 {White blocks our dark-squared bishop. We'll push the b5-knight back.} 8...a6 9.Na3 {The only move.} 9...e4 {And as in many similar positions, we gain space with ...e4. The knight will be flexible on d7 and ...b5 can be played next.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 3: White Defends d4 (3.e3) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5345e22a-43f9-4588-b4ee-dd1f1f8bb2b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third study! Let's check out another move that White could go for and that is 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White expands. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.e3 {And White decides to support the pawn with e3. The best approach is to continue fighting for the center.} 3...d5 4.Nc3 {White brings out the b1-knight, pressuring the d5-pawn. We'll have to support it.} 4...e6 5.Nf3 {White brings out the other knight. We can always have ideas of pushing ...b5 in the future once the white c-pawn is traded off, so let's prepare it with a6.} 5...a6 6.cxd5 {White doesn't allow a trade on c4 with ...b5 coming, and captures on d5. We recapture with the e-pawn.} 6...exd5 7.Be2 {White prepares to castle. Let's develop naturally.} 7...Nc6 8.O-O {White castles. Black continues their development.} 8...Bd6 9.dxc5 {White now trades on c5 so that we have to move the bishop twice.} 9...Bxc5 10.a3 {White prepares to push b4. We don't mind White getting b4. King safety is more important.} 10...O-O 11.b4 {White now expands...} 11...Ba7 {...but we retreat to a7, from where it can support the ...d4-push. We will develop our light-squared bishop to wherever we see fit, e6 or even f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 3: White Defends d4 (3.e3) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5345e22a-43f9-4588-b4ee-dd1f1f8bb2b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third study! Let's check out another move that White could go for and that is 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White expands. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.e3 {And White decides to support the pawn with e3. The best approach is to continue fighting for the center.} 3...d5 4.cxd5 {White immediately trades on d5. Recapturing with the queen would run into Nc3, with tempo, so we take with the knight.} 4...Nxd5 5.dxc5 {White grabs the pawn on c5. Let's add an attacker to it, aiming to recapture.} 5...e6 6.e4 {White kicks out our knight. We'll retreat to f6, where it attacks the e-pawn.} 6...Nf6 7.Qxd8+ {White trades the queens.} 7...Kxd8 {We recapture the queen and target the c5-pawn, and we will play Kc7 to get our king off the open d-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 3: White Defends d4 (3.e3) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5345e22a-43f9-4588-b4ee-dd1f1f8bb2b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third study! Let's check out another move that White could go for and that is 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White expands. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.e3 {And White decides to support the pawn with e3. The best approach is to continue fighting for the center.} 3...d5 4.Nc3 {White brings out the b1-knight, pressuring the d5-pawn. We'll have to support it.} 4...e6 5.Nf3 {White brings out the other knight. We can always have ideas of pushing ...b5 in the future once the white c-pawn is traded off, so let's prepare it with a6.} 5...a6 6.cxd5 {White doesn't allow a trade on c4 with ...b5 coming, and captures on d5. We recapture with the e-pawn.} 6...exd5 7.dxc5 {White releases tension in the center. We simply recapture.} 7...Bxc5 8.Be2 {White prepares to castle. Now it's time to get the king out of the center.} 8...O-O 9.O-O {White castles.} 9...Nc6 {And we bring the other knight to a better square, while the light-squared bishop will be developed either to g4, f5 or to e6 if the d5-pawn needs more protection.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 3: White Defends d4 (3.e3) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5345e22a-43f9-4588-b4ee-dd1f1f8bb2b5/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the third study! Let's check out another move that White could go for and that is 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White expands. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.e3 {And White decides to support the pawn with e3. The best approach is to continue fighting for the center.} 3...d5 4.Nf3 {White develops. Defending the c5-pawn could be problematic, so we'll trade.} 4...cxd4 5.exd4 {White recaptures with the pawn. And Black goes for a fianchetto approach.} 5...g6 6.cxd5 {White trades on d5 instead of opting for the development, which is played in 23% of the games. We'll capture and bring our knight into the center of the board.} 6...Nxd5 7.Nc3 {Now White goes for the development. Let's continue our fianchetto plan.} 7...Bg7 8.Bc4 {White puts extra pressure on our knight. Instead of retreating it, we can capture on c3.} 8...Nxc3 9.bxc3 {White recaptures.} 9...Qc7 {The queen has a nice square on c7. It puts pressure along the c-file and restricts White's dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 3: White Defends d4 (3.e3) / Variation 1"]
