1.d4 {Welcome to the third study! Let's check out another move that White could go for and that is 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White expands. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.e3 {And White decides to support the pawn with e3. The best approach is to continue fighting for the center.} 3...d5 4.Nf3 {White develops. Defending the c5-pawn could be problematic, so we'll trade.} 4...cxd4 5.exd4 {White recaptures with the pawn. And Black goes for a fianchetto approach.} 5...g6 6.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight.} 6...Bg7 {We fianchetto the bishop and proceed with developing moves like ...Nc6, ...b6, ...Bb7. The plan will revolve around pressuring White's d4-pawn and taking control over the c-file with ...Rc8.} *

