1.d4 {Welcome to the third study! Let's check out another move that White could go for and that is 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White expands. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.e3 {And White decides to support the pawn with e3. The best approach is to continue fighting for the center.} 3...d5 4.dxc5 {If White trades on c5...} 4...e6 {...we'll play ...e6 and target the c5-pawn with quick development.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 4: The Exchange Variation (3.dxc5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5623c1de-f019-496c-9e8e-a7f6d828381e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the fourth study on third-move deviations! It is the least common move, but your opponent might grab the seemingly free pawn on c5!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White gains more space. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {On the third move, White grabs the c5-pawn. This happens very rarely, but White might try to hold onto the pawn after we continue with e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops with the second most popular move, played in 20% of the games. We can safely grab the pawn now.} 4...Bxc5 5.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight. And we get our king to safety.} 5...O-O {And after ...O-O we transpose to the main line.} 6.e4 {White continues with the third most popular move, which is e4, immediately gaining space in the center. As e3 wasn't pushed, we can continue by attacking f2.} 6...Ng4 7.Nd4 {White blocks the attack by jumping to d4. Seems like they have covered the attack successfully, but Black has a tactical shot.} 7...Nxf2 8.Kxf2 {White recaptures. Now we can fork the king and knight.} 8...Qf6+ 9.Ke3 {The king defends the knight, but now we simply add an attacker.} 9...Nc6 10.Ne2 {White protects it by retreating.} 10...d5 {And since White's king is vulnerable, our main idea is to blow open up the center! With moves like ...dxc4 and ...Rd8 coming, White's position will slowly collapse.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 4: The Exchange Variation (3.dxc5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5623c1de-f019-496c-9e8e-a7f6d828381e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the fourth study on third-move deviations! It is the least common move, but your opponent might grab the seemingly free pawn on c5!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White gains more space. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {On the third move, White grabs the c5-pawn. This happens very rarely, but White might try to hold onto the pawn after we continue with e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops with the second most popular move, played in 20% of the games. We can safely grab the pawn now.} 4...Bxc5 5.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight. And we get our king to safety.} 5...O-O {And after ...O-O we transpose to the main line.} 6.e3 {White blocks our bishop on c5. We'll continue by fighting for the center.} 6...d5 7.cxd5 {White trades. We'll take back with the pawn.} 7...exd5 8.Be2 {White prepares to castle. We'll continue our development, starting with the knight.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O {White castles.} 9...Re8 {And now we improve the rook. Moves like ...Bg4 and ...a6 are very natural here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 4: The Exchange Variation (3.dxc5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5623c1de-f019-496c-9e8e-a7f6d828381e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the fourth study on third-move deviations! It is the least common move, but your opponent might grab the seemingly free pawn on c5!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White gains more space. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {On the third move, White grabs the c5-pawn. This happens very rarely, but White might try to hold onto the pawn after we continue with e6.} 3...e6 4.Nf3 {White develops with the second most popular move, played in 20% of the games. We can safely grab the pawn now.} 4...Bxc5 5.Nc3 {White develops the queenside knight. And we get our king to safety.} 5...O-O {And after ...O-O we transpose to the main line.} 6.Bg5 {White pins our knight. We'll unpin with Qb6, when we hit both f2 and b2!} 6...Qb6 7.e3 {White blocks. But we can still capture on b2.} 7...Qxb2 8.Bxf6 {White captures our knight, intending to damage our pawn structure.} 8...Bb4 {But instead of recapturing, we add an attacker to the knight! White's king doesn't feel safe.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 4: The Exchange Variation (3.dxc5) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5623c1de-f019-496c-9e8e-a7f6d828381e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the fourth study on third-move deviations! It is the least common move, but your opponent might grab the seemingly free pawn on c5!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White gains more space. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {On the third move, White grabs the c5-pawn. This happens very rarely, but White might try to hold onto the pawn after we continue with e6.} 3...e6 4.b4 {White plays b4, trying to defend the pawn on c5. This is the least popular move out of the three. Our idea is to disrupt White's pawns on the queenside.} 4...a5 5.b5 {White pushes even further, gaining space, but now the c5-pawn can be freely captured.} 5...Bxc5 6.Nf3 {White develops the knight. With the bishop on c5, we can start attacking.} 6...Ne4 7.e3 {White defends...} 7...Qf6 {...but after ...Qf6 the rook on a1 is trapped!} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 4: The Exchange Variation (3.dxc5) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5623c1de-f019-496c-9e8e-a7f6d828381e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the fourth study on third-move deviations! It is the least common move, but your opponent might grab the seemingly free pawn on c5!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White gains more space. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {On the third move, White grabs the c5-pawn. This happens very rarely, but White might try to hold onto the pawn after we continue with e6.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {White goes for 4.Nc3, the most popular move played in more than half of the games. We can just recapture on c5 and get our bishop out in the meantime.} 4...Bxc5 5.Nf3 {White develops the queenside knight. And we get our king to safety.} 5...O-O {And after ...O-O we transpose to the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 3 Deviations / Study 4: The Exchange Variation (3.dxc5) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/7f2e7c8c-9169-4914-9e47-40582611f14b/studies/5623c1de-f019-496c-9e8e-a7f6d828381e/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the fourth study on third-move deviations! It is the least common move, but your opponent might grab the seemingly free pawn on c5!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White gains more space. We reply with the Benoni.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {On the third move, White grabs the c5-pawn. This happens very rarely, but White might try to hold onto the pawn after we continue with e6.} 3...e6 4.b4 {White plays b4, trying to defend the pawn on c5. This is the least popular move out of the three. Our idea is to disrupt White's pawns on the queenside.} 4...a5 5.Bd2 {White defends the pawn with the bishop. We'll simply take on b4. White's queenside pawns are shattered.} 5...axb4 6.Bxb4 {Black recaptures.} 6...Na6 {And after ...Na6 the structure collapses.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 1: White's Unambitious 3.e3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/4771e477-225a-4f7a-9a05-ef527160b3d6/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last chapter of our Czech-Benoni course, we'll take a look at the Anti-Czech Benoni, which is characterized by 2.Nf3 by White. Some of the positions we cover in this chapter will be similar to usual Benoni structures, while others will not, which is why it's important to know. The first study will deal with 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {Already a deviation from what we've seen before. White activates the knight to f3 instead of playing c4. This is designed to throw us off our game and lead to different 1.d4 positions, but we'll keep playing in Benoni style.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White supports the center with the pawn, but this locks their bishop on c1, and we can simply grab some space in the center. This move is played 20% of the time, amounting to 700,000 games.} 3...d5 4.c3 {The most popular move in this position is 4.c3, and it's played 23% of the time. White solidifies the center with two pawns. We'll bring our queen's knight out.} 4...Nbd7 5.Bd3 {White activates their bishop to d3. It's a London-like structure, except their bishop is stuck on c1. We won't go for ..e6, as this would run into the same problem of restricting the queen's bishop. Instead, we'll prepare to fianchetto the f8-bishop.} 5...g6 6.Nbd2 {The knight gets out to d2. Let's plant the bishop on g7.} 6...Bg7 7.O-O {White castles. We will too.} 7...O-O 8.Re1 {White slides the rook to e1 and prepares to push e4. That's going to be their front of attack. Now it's time to take care of the light-squared bishop.} 8...b6 9.e4 {White pushes e4 now. Let's learn how to fight against this. We'll start by taking on d4 and reducing the sturdiness of their center.} 9...cxd4 10.cxd4 {Once White recaptures, we'll trade yet again.} 10...dxe4 11.Nxe4 {The knight recaptures.} 11...Bb7 {And suddenly our bishop is extremely strong on b7. This is an IQP position, so you should look to control the d5-square as much as possible in the near future. You might even be able to capture on d4 at some point.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 1: White's Unambitious 3.e3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/4771e477-225a-4f7a-9a05-ef527160b3d6/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last chapter of our Czech-Benoni course, we'll take a look at the Anti-Czech Benoni, which is characterized by 2.Nf3 by White. Some of the positions we cover in this chapter will be similar to usual Benoni structures, while others will not, which is why it's important to know. The first study will deal with 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {Already a deviation from what we've seen before. White activates the knight to f3 instead of playing c4. This is designed to throw us off our game and lead to different 1.d4 positions, but we'll keep playing in Benoni style.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White supports the center with the pawn, but this locks their bishop on c1, and we can simply grab some space in the center. This move is played 20% of the time, amounting to 700,000 games.} 3...d5 4.b3 {Pushing b3 is played only 9% of the time, but has a logical basis behind it. White wants to deal with the problematic dark-squared bishop by placing it on b2. We'll take on d4.} 4...cxd4 5.exd4 {White recaptures with the pawn. Usually, this would open up their dark-squared bishop, but White has already committed to the fianchetto in this line. We should continue to develop, so we'll play Nc6.} 5...Nc6 6.Bb2 {White gets their bishop to b2, as planned. However, it's only staring at the d4-pawn now! Our bishop comes to g4, pinning the f3-knight.} 6...Bg4 7.Be2 {White unpins and prepares castling. We'll prepare to get our last minor piece out.} 7...e6 8.O-O {White castles. And we'll get our bishop out in readiness for castling.} 8...Be7 9.Nbd2 {The d2-square is better for White's knight. They keep their c-pawn options open.} 9...O-O {And we'll castle. From here on, you might face Ne5, after which you should trade on e2. If not, beware of c4 possibilities for White at any point. In that case, even capturing on c4 and playing on the queenside with ...Rc8 is good.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 1: White's Unambitious 3.e3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/4771e477-225a-4f7a-9a05-ef527160b3d6/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last chapter of our Czech-Benoni course, we'll take a look at the Anti-Czech Benoni, which is characterized by 2.Nf3 by White. Some of the positions we cover in this chapter will be similar to usual Benoni structures, while others will not, which is why it's important to know. The first study will deal with 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {Already a deviation from what we've seen before. White activates the knight to f3 instead of playing c4. This is designed to throw us off our game and lead to different 1.d4 positions, but we'll keep playing in Benoni style.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White supports the center with the pawn, but this locks their bishop on c1, and we can simply grab some space in the center. This move is played 20% of the time, amounting to 700,000 games.} 3...d5 4.dxc5 {White capturing on c5 is played 13% of the time here. White wants to take the free pawn and try to hold on to it. We'll play e6, and if White doesn't defend the c5-pawn, we're simply going to activate our bishop on that square.} 4...e6 5.b4 {As mentioned, expect to see b4 here a lot. However, this way of playing lets us expand while undermining White's structure.} 5...a5 6.c3 {It's horrible for White to capture, of course. So they defend the pawn with c3. We'll take on b4, and keep trying to dismantle the white queenside pawns.} 6...axb4 7.cxb4 {Once White takes, we're not done chipping away at their structure on the queenside.} 7...b6 8.Bb5+ {White gives a check first. We can simply block the check.} 8...Bd7 9.Bxd7+ {White trades. We'll recapture with the b8-knight, adding further pressure to the pawns.} 9...Nbxd7 10.a4 {White could accept that they can no longer maintain the pawns and instead play to expand on the queenside. We'll recover our pawn.} 10...bxc5 11.b5 {They push b5 and get two connected passed pawns. This is definitely a sound plan. We'll develop our final minor piece and prepare to castle.} 11...Bd6 12.O-O {White castles. We'll castle as well.} 12...O-O 13.Bb2 {The bishop comes out to b2 now.} 13...Qc7 {And a good developing move for the queen is ...Qc7. We've got a passed pawn of our own on the c-file and we should look to push it. In the center, ...e5 is great. Just be careful not to trade too many pieces, as then White's queenside pawns can become too strong to stop.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 1: White's Unambitious 3.e3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/4771e477-225a-4f7a-9a05-ef527160b3d6/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last chapter of our Czech-Benoni course, we'll take a look at the Anti-Czech Benoni, which is characterized by 2.Nf3 by White. Some of the positions we cover in this chapter will be similar to usual Benoni structures, while others will not, which is why it's important to know. The first study will deal with 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {Already a deviation from what we've seen before. White activates the knight to f3 instead of playing c4. This is designed to throw us off our game and lead to different 1.d4 positions, but we'll keep playing in Benoni style.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White supports the center with the pawn, but this locks their bishop on c1, and we can simply grab some space in the center. This move is played 20% of the time, amounting to 700,000 games.} 3...d5 4.dxc5 {White capturing on c5 is played 13% of the time here. White wants to take the free pawn and try to hold on to it. We'll play e6, and if White doesn't defend the c5-pawn, we're simply going to activate our bishop on that square.} 4...e6 5.b4 {As mentioned, expect to see b4 here a lot. However, this way of playing lets us expand while undermining White's structure.} 5...a5 6.c3 {It's horrible for White to capture, of course. So they defend the pawn with c3. We'll take on b4, and keep trying to dismantle the white queenside pawns.} 6...axb4 7.cxb4 {Once White takes, we're not done chipping away at their structure on the queenside.} 7...b6 8.Bb5+ {White gives a check first. We can simply block the check.} 8...Bd7 9.Bxd7+ {White trades. We'll recapture with the b8-knight, adding further pressure to the pawns.} 9...Nbxd7 10.c6 {One of the options is to push the pawn and attack our knight. But remember - that pawn was pinned to the b4-pawn! Capturing is with check!} 10...Bxb4+ 11.Bd2 {White blocks with the bishop. We'll exchange bishops.} 11...Bxd2+ 12.Nbxd2 {The knight recaptures. Now our knight is still under attack, so we move it forward, pointing aggressively at the e4-square.} 12...Nc5 13.O-O {Once they castle...} 13...Qc7 {You can comfortably place the queen on c7 and capture the pawn next. In the center, the e5-square is free and you should definitely push to grab more space. White capturing on c5 has backfired in this instance; Black is much better, if not winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 1: White's Unambitious 3.e3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/4771e477-225a-4f7a-9a05-ef527160b3d6/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last chapter of our Czech-Benoni course, we'll take a look at the Anti-Czech Benoni, which is characterized by 2.Nf3 by White. Some of the positions we cover in this chapter will be similar to usual Benoni structures, while others will not, which is why it's important to know. The first study will deal with 3.e3.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {Already a deviation from what we've seen before. White activates the knight to f3 instead of playing c4. This is designed to throw us off our game and lead to different 1.d4 positions, but we'll keep playing in Benoni style.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White supports the center with the pawn, but this locks their bishop on c1, and we can simply grab some space in the center. This move is played 20% of the time, amounting to 700,000 games.} 3...d5 4.c4 {The move c4 is played 18% of the time in this position. White creates a lot of tension in the center and we have an important decision to make. We'll capture on d4.} 4...cxd4 5.exd4 {White recaptures with the pawn.} 5...g6 {And I recommend ...g6 here, looking to play ...Bg7. This position actually resembles the Panov Caro-Kann. If White captures on d5, even a maneuver like ...Nbd7-Nb6 to slowly get it back is fine.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 2: An Early 3.d5 Advance / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/daa385b6-0712-4cfb-ac6b-e32dde31cfac/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of the Anti-Czech Benoni chapter, we'll take a look at White playing d5 on move 3. This approach resembles our known Benoni more than the first study, with some differences that we'll analyze.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 instead of playing c4 first. We'll strike at the white center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White pushes d5 and grabs space. This prevents ...d5 from us in the future. This move is played 17% of the time. We'll reply with d6. This ensures that White cannot later overrun our position with e4-e5.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {White developing the knight to c3 is the second most popular move here, played about 20% of the time. To develop the dark-squared bishop, we'll opt for the fianchetto.} 4...g6 5.e4 {White continues in an active manner with e4. Let's continue our plan.} 5...Bg7 6.Bb5+ {The most popular choice is the active check on b5, which is played 20% of the time. We'll just block with the bishop, as the knight will be useful later.} 6...Bd7 7.a4 {White could choose to protect the bishop instead of trading. The idea is that they want a pawn on b5 to prevent our future queenside expansion. We'll castle.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {They might do the same. Now, we should evaluate whether a trade on b5 is good for us or not. If we trade, sure, White will have a strong pawn on b5, but that pawn will also be over-extended. We'll then be able to undermine it with ...a6 and grab a lot of space on the queenside.} 8...Bxb5 9.axb5 {White recaptures with the pawn. We continue to develop.} 9...Nbd7 10.h3 {White goes for h3, which is potentially useful to keep our knight out of g4. We'll challenge on the queenside.} 10...a6 11.bxa6 {White captures... And we will take back with the rook.} 11...Rxa6 12.Rxa6 {White takes on a6, and naturally we recapture.} 12...bxa6 13.Qe2 {White might go after that pawn on a6, but we've got a nice active move to defend it.} 13...Qa5 {And that's bringing the queen to a5. As mentioned, we've got a bunch of space and potential on the queenside. We'll use it by bringing the knight from f6 all the way to b5 and trying to trade it for White's knight on c3, which would connect our pawns again. The rook can slide to b8.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 2: An Early 3.d5 Advance / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/daa385b6-0712-4cfb-ac6b-e32dde31cfac/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of the Anti-Czech Benoni chapter, we'll take a look at White playing d5 on move 3. This approach resembles our known Benoni more than the first study, with some differences that we'll analyze.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 instead of playing c4 first. We'll strike at the white center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White pushes d5 and grabs space. This prevents ...d5 from us in the future. This move is played 17% of the time. We'll reply with d6. This ensures that White cannot later overrun our position with e4-e5.} 3...d6 4.c4 {If White plays c4 here, the position will most likely transpose to a chapter we've already covered. That's why this move is played 61% of the time. We'll return to our main idea of playing e5 and forming a strong mass of central pawns.} 4...e5 5.dxe6 {White can capture en passant and we don't have the option of recapturing with the d-pawn.} 5...fxe6 6.Nc3 {White activates the knight to c3 now. We continue our development.} 6...Be7 7.e4 {Expect to see e4 from White a lot. They should gain space when they can. We'll fight for the center ourselves.} 7...Nc6 8.Be2 {The bishop comes out to e2 now. Time to castle.} 8...O-O 9.O-O {White castles as well.} 9...e5 {And we'll play ...e5. We give up the d5-square but get a clamp on the d4-square. This square trade-off in the center is usually fine for either side. We'll shove our knight into d4 and White will probably jump to d5.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 2: An Early 3.d5 Advance / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/daa385b6-0712-4cfb-ac6b-e32dde31cfac/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of the Anti-Czech Benoni chapter, we'll take a look at White playing d5 on move 3. This approach resembles our known Benoni more than the first study, with some differences that we'll analyze.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 instead of playing c4 first. We'll strike at the white center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White pushes d5 and grabs space. This prevents ...d5 from us in the future. This move is played 17% of the time. We'll reply with d6. This ensures that White cannot later overrun our position with e4-e5.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {White developing the knight to c3 is the second most popular move here, played about 20% of the time. To develop the dark-squared bishop, we'll opt for the fianchetto.} 4...g6 5.e4 {White continues in an active manner with e4. Let's continue our plan.} 5...Bg7 6.Bb5+ {The most popular choice is the active check on b5, which is played 20% of the time. We'll just block with the bishop, as the knight will be useful later.} 6...Bd7 7.Bxd7+ {A trade is definitely logical. This does kind of solve White's light-squared bishop problems, but our bishop wasn't exactly active either.} 7...Nbxd7 8.O-O {White castles. We'll get our king into safety as well.} 8...O-O 9.Re1 {The rook slides to e1 to support the center. The center is closed, our king is on the kingside, but we've got a lot of space on the queenside, and we'll prepare to expand with a later ...b5.} 9...a6 10.a4 {White stops it with a4. But at the very least we now know where we should focus our play on.} 10...Ne8 {That's why ...Ne8 is logical. We'll reroute the knight to c7 and slide the rook to b8. After that, White won't be able to stop ...b5! Once we achieve that push, we'll be extremely strong on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 2: An Early 3.d5 Advance / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/daa385b6-0712-4cfb-ac6b-e32dde31cfac/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of the Anti-Czech Benoni chapter, we'll take a look at White playing d5 on move 3. This approach resembles our known Benoni more than the first study, with some differences that we'll analyze.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 instead of playing c4 first. We'll strike at the white center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White pushes d5 and grabs space. This prevents ...d5 from us in the future. This move is played 17% of the time. We'll reply with d6. This ensures that White cannot later overrun our position with e4-e5.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {White developing the knight to c3 is the second most popular move here, played about 20% of the time. To develop the dark-squared bishop, we'll opt for the fianchetto.} 4...g6 5.e4 {White continues in an active manner with e4. Let's continue our plan.} 5...Bg7 6.Bd3 {White players activate the bishop to d3 about 15% of the time. It's definitely unorthodox to activate the bishop like this, staring at its own pawn. We'll castle.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White does the same. We could get the light-squared bishop out to g4 before we play ...e6, but there's not much merit to trading on f3 now, so instead we'll first play to undermine the white center.} 7...e6 8.Bg5 {White activates their bishop to g5 now, pinning our knight. They don't want to capture on e6. We'll challenge the bishop.} 8...h6 9.Bh4 {It will probably move to h4, after which we can continue with our central plan and capture on d5.} 9...exd5 10.exd5 {White recaptures with the pawn. Don't be afraid if they recapture on d5 with the knight. Your knight on f6 is safe as you can always play ...g5. Now we'll pin the f3-knight.} 10...Bg4 11.h3 {White attacks our bishop, and we'll capture on f3 in preparation for bringing a knight to e5.} 11...Bxf3 12.Qxf3 {The queen recaptures...} 12...Nbd7 {And we'll develop the knight, eyeing that e5-square. The trade on f3 came in handy after already having traded the pawns on d5. Now the position is somewhat more comfortable to play for us.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 2: An Early 3.d5 Advance / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/daa385b6-0712-4cfb-ac6b-e32dde31cfac/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of the Anti-Czech Benoni chapter, we'll take a look at White playing d5 on move 3. This approach resembles our known Benoni more than the first study, with some differences that we'll analyze.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 instead of playing c4 first. We'll strike at the white center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White pushes d5 and grabs space. This prevents ...d5 from us in the future. This move is played 17% of the time. We'll reply with d6. This ensures that White cannot later overrun our position with e4-e5.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {White developing the knight to c3 is the second most popular move here, played about 20% of the time. To develop the dark-squared bishop, we'll opt for the fianchetto.} 4...g6 5.e4 {White continues in an active manner with e4. Let's continue our plan.} 5...Bg7 6.Be2 {The passive-looking 6.Be2 is played just under 20% of the time here. White wants to castle. We'll get our king into safety.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {And so will they. A common motif in this study is undermining White's central structure with ...e6.} 7...e6 8.Bg5 {A move like Bg5 is definitely sensible, pinning our knight. We challenge the bishop by playing h6.} 8...h6 9.Bh4 {White retreats to h4, maintaining the pin. We'll finally break the pin by playing g5.} 9...g5 10.Bg3 {The g3-square is the only possible one to retreat to. Now we'll capture on d5.} 10...exd5 11.exd5 {White recaptures.} 11...Nh5 {And now we'll go after the bishop. This was the point of playing ...g5, as we'll capture that active bishop now. Meanwhile, our bishop on g7 is standing as strong as ever.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 2: An Early 3.d5 Advance / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/daa385b6-0712-4cfb-ac6b-e32dde31cfac/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of the Anti-Czech Benoni chapter, we'll take a look at White playing d5 on move 3. This approach resembles our known Benoni more than the first study, with some differences that we'll analyze.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 instead of playing c4 first. We'll strike at the white center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White pushes d5 and grabs space. This prevents ...d5 from us in the future. This move is played 17% of the time. We'll reply with d6. This ensures that White cannot later overrun our position with e4-e5.} 3...d6 4.Nc3 {White developing the knight to c3 is the second most popular move here, played about 20% of the time. To develop the dark-squared bishop, we'll opt for the fianchetto.} 4...g6 5.e4 {White continues in an active manner with e4. Let's continue our plan.} 5...Bg7 6.Be2 {The passive-looking 6.Be2 is played just under 20% of the time here. White wants to castle. We'll get our king into safety.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {And so will they. A common motif in this study is undermining White's central structure with ...e6.} 7...e6 8.dxe6 {If White takes in any way...} 8...Bxe6 {We'll recapture with the bishop and this suddenly brings life to our position. We'll activate the knight and play for ...d5. It's not the best for White to take on e6.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 2: An Early 3.d5 Advance / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/daa385b6-0712-4cfb-ac6b-e32dde31cfac/lines"]

1.d4 {In the second study of the Anti-Czech Benoni chapter, we'll take a look at White playing d5 on move 3. This approach resembles our known Benoni more than the first study, with some differences that we'll analyze.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 instead of playing c4 first. We'll strike at the white center with c5.} 2...c5 3.d5 {White pushes d5 and grabs space. This prevents ...d5 from us in the future. This move is played 17% of the time. We'll reply with d6. This ensures that White cannot later overrun our position with e4-e5.} 3...d6 4.c4 {If White plays c4 here, the position will most likely transpose to a chapter we've already covered. That's why this move is played 61% of the time. We'll return to our main idea of playing e5 and forming a strong mass of central pawns.} 4...e5 5.Nc3 {White might choose not to capture en passant but rather develop the knight to c3. Let's continue playing standard Benoni moves.} 5...Be7 6.e4 {White advances in the center...} 6...Nbd7 {And we'll get our knight out to d7. A familiar position.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 3: Defending The Center With 3.c3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/653b61a3-760d-4c66-9eb8-2107435fb7f1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study of this chapter, we'll take a look at the 3.c3 variation by White. There is definitely merit to playing like this as it is logical to support the center with a pawn.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 now. We challenge the white d-pawn with c5.} 2...c5 3.c3 {And the move 3.c3 is played 16% of the time here, or in almost 580,000 games. This does restrict the development of the b-knight, however. Black can fight for the center.} 3...d5 4.Bf4 {This is the perk of 3.c3. White gets to activate the dark-squared bishop to f4. On the other hand, a lot of the times when the bishop is activated like this, it leaves the b2-pawn undefended.} 4...Qb6 5.Qb3 {The most confrontational move played 47% of the time is Qb3. White doesn't want to allow easy targets and offers a trade of queens. We'll ignore this trade and opt for developing moves instead.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {White opens up the light-squared bishop. A great way to gain space on the queenside without creating any weaknesses is to play c4.} 6...c4 7.Qxb6 {Be prepared for White to capture your queen at any point. We recapture, opening the a-file for our rook.} 7...axb6 8.Nbd2 {And now the knight comes to d2. Not much else White can do. The doubled b-pawns are an asset rather than a weakness. We'll push forward with b5, targeting the white pawn chain.} 8...b5 9.Be2 {White develops the bishop to e2.} 9...e6 {To play ...b4, we first have to prepare it. And a move like ...e6 is a great way to do so, as our bishop now supports the square as well.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 3: Defending The Center With 3.c3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/653b61a3-760d-4c66-9eb8-2107435fb7f1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study of this chapter, we'll take a look at the 3.c3 variation by White. There is definitely merit to playing like this as it is logical to support the center with a pawn.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 now. We challenge the white d-pawn with c5.} 2...c5 3.c3 {And the move 3.c3 is played 16% of the time here, or in almost 580,000 games. This does restrict the development of the b-knight, however. Black can fight for the center.} 3...d5 4.Bf4 {This is the perk of 3.c3. White gets to activate the dark-squared bishop to f4. On the other hand, a lot of the times when the bishop is activated like this, it leaves the b2-pawn undefended.} 4...Qb6 5.Qb3 {The most confrontational move played 47% of the time is Qb3. White doesn't want to allow easy targets and offers a trade of queens. We'll ignore this trade and opt for developing moves instead.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {White opens up the light-squared bishop. A great way to gain space on the queenside without creating any weaknesses is to play c4.} 6...c4 7.Qc2 {White could choose to retreat here, but this gives us the upper hand. It's like them admitting their mistake. We've got a tactic here.} 7...Bf5 8.Qc1 {A more sensible move by White is retreating to c1. But now their queen is extremely passive. Let's go after their active piece.} 8...Nh5 9.Bg3 {If White chooses to retreat right away, we can just take that bishop.} 9...Nxg3 10.hxg3 {The h-pawn recaptures. And now we cover the h-file.} 10...h6 11.Nbd2 {White finally develops the knight, but it doesn't have much potential.} 11...e6 {And we'll simply continue with ...e6. Now Nh4 isn't effective anymore. White's pieces are completely cramped together with little to no prospects of immediate improvement.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 3: Defending The Center With 3.c3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/653b61a3-760d-4c66-9eb8-2107435fb7f1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study of this chapter, we'll take a look at the 3.c3 variation by White. There is definitely merit to playing like this as it is logical to support the center with a pawn.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 now. We challenge the white d-pawn with c5.} 2...c5 3.c3 {And the move 3.c3 is played 16% of the time here, or in almost 580,000 games. This does restrict the development of the b-knight, however. Black can fight for the center.} 3...d5 4.Bf4 {This is the perk of 3.c3. White gets to activate the dark-squared bishop to f4. On the other hand, a lot of the times when the bishop is activated like this, it leaves the b2-pawn undefended.} 4...Qb6 5.Qb3 {The most confrontational move played 47% of the time is Qb3. White doesn't want to allow easy targets and offers a trade of queens. We'll ignore this trade and opt for developing moves instead.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {White opens up the light-squared bishop. A great way to gain space on the queenside without creating any weaknesses is to play c4.} 6...c4 7.Qc2 {White could choose to retreat here, but this gives us the upper hand. It's like them admitting their mistake. We've got a tactic here.} 7...Bf5 8.Qc1 {A more sensible move by White is retreating to c1. But now their queen is extremely passive. Let's go after their active piece.} 8...Nh5 9.Bg5 {They can choose to slide to g5 here. However, that just gives us more tempi.} 9...h6 10.Bh4 {White retreats to h4. We can push even further!} 10...g5 11.Bg3 {It has to retreat to g3 now. It's a strong bishop. Getting rid of it is only logical.} 11...Nxg3 12.hxg3 {The h-pawn recaptures, and remember to be careful here if you reach this position. White is threatening to capture the g5-pawn, as its defender is pinned to the rook.} 12...Bg7 {But the simple fianchetto is sufficient defense here. Black is much better because of the clear advantage in activity.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 3: Defending The Center With 3.c3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/653b61a3-760d-4c66-9eb8-2107435fb7f1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study of this chapter, we'll take a look at the 3.c3 variation by White. There is definitely merit to playing like this as it is logical to support the center with a pawn.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 now. We challenge the white d-pawn with c5.} 2...c5 3.c3 {And the move 3.c3 is played 16% of the time here, or in almost 580,000 games. This does restrict the development of the b-knight, however. Black can fight for the center.} 3...d5 4.Bf4 {This is the perk of 3.c3. White gets to activate the dark-squared bishop to f4. On the other hand, a lot of the times when the bishop is activated like this, it leaves the b2-pawn undefended.} 4...Qb6 5.Qb3 {The most confrontational move played 47% of the time is Qb3. White doesn't want to allow easy targets and offers a trade of queens. We'll ignore this trade and opt for developing moves instead.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {White opens up the light-squared bishop. A great way to gain space on the queenside without creating any weaknesses is to play c4.} 6...c4 7.Qc2 {White could choose to retreat here, but this gives us the upper hand. It's like them admitting their mistake. We've got a tactic here.} 7...Bf5 8.Qxf5 {If White takes it, we're winning.} 8...Qxb2 {We'll simply capture on b2 and they can't save the rook. White can try being tricky with 9.Be2, the idea being 9...Qxa1 10.Qc2 and our queen is in trouble, but the insertion of the equally crafty 9...g6 (or e6) means their queen can't get back to c2 in order to corral ours!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 3: Defending The Center With 3.c3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/653b61a3-760d-4c66-9eb8-2107435fb7f1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study of this chapter, we'll take a look at the 3.c3 variation by White. There is definitely merit to playing like this as it is logical to support the center with a pawn.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 now. We challenge the white d-pawn with c5.} 2...c5 3.c3 {And the move 3.c3 is played 16% of the time here, or in almost 580,000 games. This does restrict the development of the b-knight, however. Black can fight for the center.} 3...d5 4.Bf4 {This is the perk of 3.c3. White gets to activate the dark-squared bishop to f4. On the other hand, a lot of the times when the bishop is activated like this, it leaves the b2-pawn undefended.} 4...Qb6 5.Qc1 {The move Qc1 is played only 8% of the time, and it seems really passive. White does defend the pawn but allows for easy development by us.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {Once White strengthens the center...} 6...Bf5 {We'll activate the bishop as well. Next, we'll look to play ...e6 and bring the rook to the c-file where White's queen is located. Once the queenside is fully developed, we can focus on the kingside.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 3: Defending The Center With 3.c3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/653b61a3-760d-4c66-9eb8-2107435fb7f1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study of this chapter, we'll take a look at the 3.c3 variation by White. There is definitely merit to playing like this as it is logical to support the center with a pawn.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 now. We challenge the white d-pawn with c5.} 2...c5 3.c3 {And the move 3.c3 is played 16% of the time here, or in almost 580,000 games. This does restrict the development of the b-knight, however. Black can fight for the center.} 3...d5 4.Bf4 {This is the perk of 3.c3. White gets to activate the dark-squared bishop to f4. On the other hand, a lot of the times when the bishop is activated like this, it leaves the b2-pawn undefended.} 4...Qb6 5.Qc2 {You'll face Qc2 22% of the time here, and it does look logical. White activates the queen and protects the pawn in a non-confrontational manner. We'll develop and put pressure on d4.} 5...Nc6 6.e3 {In this instance, when White defends the center with e3...} 6...g6 {I recommend going for ...g6. This will allow for our dark-squared bishop to attack the center from g7. The other benefit of this move is that the g-pawn supports the f5-square for the bishop to develop to and target the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 3: Defending The Center With 3.c3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/653b61a3-760d-4c66-9eb8-2107435fb7f1/lines"]

1.d4 {In the third study of this chapter, we'll take a look at the 3.c3 variation by White. There is definitely merit to playing like this as it is logical to support the center with a pawn.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {White develops to f3 now. We challenge the white d-pawn with c5.} 2...c5 3.c3 {And the move 3.c3 is played 16% of the time here, or in almost 580,000 games. This does restrict the development of the b-knight, however. Black can fight for the center.} 3...d5 4.Bf4 {This is the perk of 3.c3. White gets to activate the dark-squared bishop to f4. On the other hand, a lot of the times when the bishop is activated like this, it leaves the b2-pawn undefended.} 4...Qb6 5.b3 {A way to save the pawn is certainly to push it, and White players do this 14% of the time. The downside of this move is that it weakens White's queenside structure. We'll take on d4, creating a slight weakness for White on b4.} 5...cxd4 6.cxd4 {Once White recaptures... We'll develop our queenside pieces.} 6...Nc6 7.e3 {The d-pawn is easily protected with e3, but we have nothing to worry about in this position.} 7...Bf5 {We'll bring the bishop to f5 and the upcoming moves seem natural. We want to centralize the knight on the e4-square and open up the dark-squared bishop with ...e6. White's b3-move has weakened the dark squares, so our dark-squared bishop could be a monster.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 4: White Plays 3.dxc5 and 3.Bf4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/12087c8f-d4d9-4e32-abcb-331e5d9375a6/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last study, we'll look at the two remaining options for White one move 3: 3.dxc5 and 3.Bf4. Both of these moves are rare compared to others, and for a reason. White abstains from logical play in an attempt to spice up the position. It often backfires.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll play the signature Benoni move of c5.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {White capturing the pawn certainly seems dubious, as they abandon the center. This move is still played 9% of the time, in just under 350,000 games. We'll open up our dark-squared bishop with the intention of recapturing the pawn.} 3...e6 4.b4 {If they do protect the pawn, you've probably seen this common motif in many different openings. a5 starts to undermine the pawns.} 4...a5 5.c3 {White defends with c3, but they're not out of the woods yet. We take on b4.} 5...axb4 6.cxb4 {White recaptures. We'll develop with tempo, hitting b4.} 6...Nc6 7.Qb3 {They can defend with the queen.} 7...b6 {In which case we can continue to chip away at that structure even further. White is hardly going to be able to hold everything together.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 4: White Plays 3.dxc5 and 3.Bf4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/12087c8f-d4d9-4e32-abcb-331e5d9375a6/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last study, we'll look at the two remaining options for White one move 3: 3.dxc5 and 3.Bf4. Both of these moves are rare compared to others, and for a reason. White abstains from logical play in an attempt to spice up the position. It often backfires.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll play the signature Benoni move of c5.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {White capturing the pawn certainly seems dubious, as they abandon the center. This move is still played 9% of the time, in just under 350,000 games. We'll open up our dark-squared bishop with the intention of recapturing the pawn.} 3...e6 4.Nc3 {If they just continue developing, we'll recapture the pawn.} 4...Bxc5 5.Bg5 {If they develop the bishop, for example, thinking it's a natural developing move, there's already trouble.} 5...Qb6 {We can bring the queen out and fork the two pawns on f2 and b2. White will lose one of them and will probably have to defend for the rest of the game.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: All 2.Nf3 Sidelines / Study 4: White Plays 3.dxc5 and 3.Bf4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/6bbb88ea-57b9-41a7-abc2-2d3e4e0c7ddc/chapters/a529acd8-09e4-48f3-ab78-758fbf07bd22/studies/12087c8f-d4d9-4e32-abcb-331e5d9375a6/lines"]

1.d4 {In the last study, we'll look at the two remaining options for White one move 3: 3.dxc5 and 3.Bf4. Both of these moves are rare compared to others, and for a reason. White abstains from logical play in an attempt to spice up the position. It often backfires.} 1...Nf6 2.Nf3 {The knight comes out to f3. We'll play the signature Benoni move of c5.} 2...c5 3.Bf4 {Developing the bishop to f4 can prove to be extremely dangerous if White doesn't know what they're doing. It's played 9% of the time. We'll take on d4, and set a little trap.} 3...cxd4 4.Nxd4 {If White recaptures with the knight, they are already lost. We have a pawn fork!} 4...e5 5.Bxe5 {The bishop captures on e5.} 5...Qa5+ {But there's another fork, and we'll win a piece. This is what you get when you don't stick to known theory!} *